2 CONT
2 CONT Gravlagd 	Kristkyrkja i Bergen
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon III Sverresson (ca. 1170–1. januar 1204) gamalnorsk Hákon Sverrisson, var konge av Noreg frå 1202 til 1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT Innhaldsliste
2 CONT
2 CONT [vis]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Bakgrunn
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Strid
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Død
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Grav og ettermæle
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 Kjelder
2 CONT
2 CONT Bakgrunn [endre]
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon vart fødd som den andre uekte sonen til den framtidige kong Sverre, truleg på Færøyane, og frilla hans Astrid Roesdotter, ein gong på 1170-talet. Dette var under den norske borgarkrigstida.
2 CONT
2 CONT Borgarkrigstida i Noreg varte frå om lag 1130 til 1240. I denne tida pågjekk det mange ulike strider av ulikt omfang og styrke. Bakgrunnen for desse stridene var dei uklare norske tronfylgjelovene, sosiale forhold og kampen mellom
2 CONC  ulike aristokratiske parti og mellom kyrkja og kongen. Det var for det meste to ulike flokkar, kjent ved ulike navn. Til slutt sto striden mellom birkebeinar og baglar. Midtpunktet for flokkane var som oftast ein kongsson som var
2 CONC t sett opp som kongsemnet til flokken. Sverre og Håkon var leiarar for birkebeinane.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strid [endre]
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon vert første gongen nemnt som ein av leiarane for faren sine styrkar i eit slag mot baglarane i Oslo i 1197. Seinare er han nemnt fleire gonger som ein av hærleiarane til Sverre. Då Sverre døydde, 9. mars 1202, erklærte han p
2 CONC å dødsleiet at han ikkje hadde andre levande søner enn Håkon. Han skreiv òg eit brev til Håkon der han rådde han til å koma til forlik med kyrkja, som Sverre hadde vore i strid med gjennom heile regjeringstida si. Då meldinga om a
2 CONC t Sverre var død nådde Håkon i Nidaros vart han tatt som hovding av dei birkebeinane som var der. Same våren vart han hylla som konge på Øyratinget.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den same våren kom dei norske biskopane, som hadde vore i eksil i Sverige og Danmark og støtta baglarane, attende til Noreg og kom til forlik med Håkon. Det verkar som om han har gitt etter for dei fleste av krava deira. Noreg var
2 CONC t løyst frå interdiktet som landet vart sett under under kong Sverre. Sagaen seier at Håkon for mildt fram og var godt likt av bøndene, og då striden mot kyrkja òg var løyst mista baglarane mykje støtte. Hausten 1202 vart baglarko
2 CONC ngen Inge Magnusson drepen av opplandsbøndene på Helgøya i Mjøsa og baglarflokken i Noreg gjekk i oppløysing. Eit nytt kongsemne, Erling Steinvegg, dukka snart opp i Danmark, men han ville ikkje reisa til Noreg fordi han såg liten
2 CONC  sjanse til å lukkast mot Håkon. Håkon var dermed blitt einekonge i Noreg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Død [endre]
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon hadde, ifylgje sagaen, eit anstrengt forhold til faren Sverre si dronning, Margret Eiriksdotter. Då Sverre døydde ville Margret ta med seg dottera deira, Kristin, til Götaland, der ho kom frå. Håkon sine menn tok kongsdotter
2 CONC a frå Margret med makt, fordi Håkon ville ha henne hjå seg. Etter kvart skal Margret ha kome til ei ordning med Håkon og fylgt han.
2 CONT
2 CONT I jula 1203 vart Håkon sjuk etter ei årelating. Han døydde 1. januar 1204. Margret kom snart under mistanke for å ha forgifta han. Ho prøvde å reinvaska seg ved å la ein av mennene sine bera jarn på hennar vegne, men mannen vart s
2 CONC terkt forbrent. Dette vart tatt som prov på at ho var skuldig, og ho måtte flykta til Sverige.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon Sverresson var ikkje gift, og då han døydde hadde han ingen kjente arvingar. Han vart difor etterfylgt av den fire år gamle nevøen sin, Guttorm Sigurdsson. Men etter at Håkon var død kom ei kvinne, Inga frå Varteig, som Håko
2 CONC n hadde hatt som frille ei tid i 1203, til birkebeinane med ein gut som ho sa var Håkon sin son. Guten hadde vorte føddi det som i dag er Eidsberg kommune etter Håkon sin død. Inga vart trudd, og sonen hennar vart seinare kong Håk
2 CONC on Håkonsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT I løpet av den korte regjeringstida si klarde Håkon å få Noreg løyst frå det kyrkjelege interdiktet og avslutta borgarkrigane, iallfall for ei tid. Om freden ville ha vart dersom Håkon hadde fått leva er umogleg å seie. Slik som d
2 CONC et gjekk så utløyste hans tidlige død ei ny oppblussing av kampane. Baglaren Erling Steinvegg såg sjansen sin, og berre nokre månader etter at Håkon døydde hadde han reist ein flokk og kome til Noreg for å krevja trona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grav og ettermæle [endre]
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon vart gravlagd i Kristkyrkja i Bergen. Denne kyrkja vart riven i 1531. Staden der ho stod, på området til det som i dag er Bergenhus festning, er merka av med eit minnesmerke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hovudkjeldene våre til Håkon sitt liv er Sverressoga og Baglarsoga, som begge vart skrivne kort tid etter hendingane dei omtalar, tidleg på 1200-talet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Føregjengar:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sverre Sigurdsson  	Konge av Noreg
2 CONT
2 CONT (1202–1204)
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Etterfølgjar:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Guttorm Sigurdsson 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kjelder [endre]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Noregs kongesoger bind 3: Sverre-soga (omsett av Halvdan Koht) og Soga om baglarar og birkebeinar (omsett av Gunnar Pedersen). Oslo, Samlaget 1979
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Haakon III of Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon III (1170s–1 January 1204), (Norwegian Håkon Sverresson, Old Norse Hákon Sverrisson) was king of Norway from 1202–1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born as the second illegitimate son of the future king Sverre, then a Faroese adventurer, by his concubine Astrid Roesdatter, at some point in the 1170s. This was during the Norwegian civil wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT The civil wars period of Norwegian history lasted from 1130 to 1240. During this period there were several interlocked conflicts of varying scale and intensity. The background for these conflicts were the unclear Norwegian success
2 CONC ion laws, social conditions and the struggle between different aristocratic parties and between Church and King. There were then two main parties, firstly known by varying names or no names at all, but finally condensed into the p
2 CONC arties of Bagler and Birkebeiner. The rallying point regularly was a royal son, who was set up as the leader of the party in question, to oppose the rule of king from the contesting party. Sverre and Håkon were leaders of the Birk
2 CONC ebeiner party.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon is first mentioned as one of the leaders of his father's armies in a battle against the Bagler in Oslo in 1197. Subsequently he is mentioned several times as taking part in his father's wars against the Bagler. On his deathb
2 CONC ed his father, who died on 9 March 1202, declared that he had no other son alive than Håkon. He also wrote a letter to Håkon advising him to settle the longstanding dispute with the church. When the news of Sverre's death reached 
2 CONC Håkon and the Birkebeiner assembled in Nidaros, Håkon was first taken as chieftain by the Birkebeiner. The same spring he was taken as king at the thing in Nidaros.
2 CONT
2 CONT The same spring the Norwegian bishops, who had been in exile in Sweden and Denmark and had supported the Bagler, returned to Norway and made a settlement with Håkon. It seems likely that he gave in to most of their demands. Norway
2 CONC  was released from the interdict it had been placed under during the reign of Sverre. Håkon is said to have been on friendly terms with the farmers and the common people, and the Bagler party soon lost much of its support. In the 
2 CONC autumn of 1202, the Bagler king Inge Magnusson was killed by the local farmers of Oppland and the Bagler party in Norway was dissolved. A new Bagler pretender, Erling Steinvegg, soon appeared in Denmark, but declined to renew the 
2 CONC fighting, as he saw little chance of succeeding against Håkon. Håkon was thus the undisputed ruler of the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon appears to have had a troubled relationship with his father's queen, Margareta Eriksdotter. After Sverre's death, Margareta attempted to return to her native Sweden with her daughter by Sverre, Kristina. Håkon's men forcibly
2 CONC  separated her from her daughter, as he wanted to keep her at his court. Subsequently, Margareta seemingly settled with Håkon and went to his court. During Christmas in 1203, Håkon fell ill after a bloodletting, and on 1 January 1
2 CONC 204 he died. His death was suspected as poisoning and his stepmother Margareta was suspected of the crime. In the end she had one of her men undergo a trial by ordeal on her behalf to prove her innocence, but the man was badly bur
2 CONC ned. This was taken as proof of her guilt, and she had to flee back to Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon Sverresson was not married, and at his death no heirs were known. He was therefore succeeded by his 4-year-old nephew Guttorm Sigurdsson. But after his death a woman, Inga of Varteig, whom Håkon had taken as a concubine for 
2 CONC a time in 1203, appeared at the Birkebeiner court with an infant son who she claimed was Håkon's son. The child had been born in present-day Østfold after the death of the putative father. The boy, named Håkon after his father, la
2 CONC ter became king Håkon IV. In the summer of 1218, Inga underwent a successful trial by ordeal (bore iron) in Bergen to show the paternity of her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Håkon's brief reign, he managed to release Norway from the church's interdict, and end the civil wars, at least for a time. Whether the peace would have lasted if he had been allowed to live is impossible to say. As it turn
2 CONC ed out, his early death sparked a renewal of the fighting, as the bagler pretender Erling Steinvegg in a matter of months gathered an army and went to Norway to claim the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon was buried in the old cathedral in Bergen. The cathedral was demolished in 1531, and the site is today marked by a memorial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our main sources to Håkon's life are Sverris saga and the Bagler sagas, both written shortly after the events, in the 13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A5kon_III_of_Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon III (Norwegian Håkon Sverresson, Old Norse Hákon Sverrisson) (1182 – 1 January 1204) was king of Norway from 1202 to 1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born as the second illegitimate son of the future king Sverre, then a Faroese adventurer, by his concubine Astrid Roesdatter, at some point in the 1170s. This was during the Norwegian civil wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT The civil war period of Norwegian history lasted from 1130 to 1240. During this period there were several interlocked conflicts of varying scale and intensity. The background for these conflicts were the unclear Norwegian successi
2 CONC on laws, social conditions and the struggle between different aristocratic parties and between Church and King. There were then two main parties, firstly known by varying names or no names at all, but finally condensed into the pa
2 CONC rties of Bagler and Birkebeiner. The rallying point regularly was a royal son, who was set up as the leader of the party in question, to oppose the rule of king from the contesting party. Sverre and Håkon were leaders of the Birke
2 CONC beiner party.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon is first mentioned as one of the leaders of his father's armies in a battle against the Bagler in Oslo in 1197. Subsequently he is mentioned several times as taking part in his father's wars against the Bagler. On his deathb
2 CONC ed his father, who died on 9 March 1202, declared that he had no other son alive than Håkon. He also wrote a letter to Håkon advising him to settle the longstanding dispute with the church. When the news of Sverre's death reached 
2 CONC Håkon and the Birkebeiner assembled in Nidaros, Håkon was first taken as chieftain by the Birkebeiner. The same spring he was taken as king at the thing in Nidaros.
2 CONT
2 CONT The same spring the Norwegian bishops, who had been in exile in Sweden and Denmark and had supported the Bagler, returned to Norway and made a settlement with Håkon. It seems likely that he gave in to most of their demands. Norway
2 CONC  was released from the interdict it had been placed under during the reign of Sverre. Håkon is said to have been on friendly terms with the farmers and the common people, and the Bagler party soon lost much of its support. In the 
2 CONC autumn of 1202, the Bagler king Inge Magnusson was killed by the local farmers of Oppland and the Bagler party in Norway was dissolved. A new Bagler pretender, Erling Steinvegg, soon appeared in Denmark, but declined to renew the 
2 CONC fighting, as he saw little chance of succeeding against Håkon. Håkon was thus the undisputed ruler of the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon appears to have had a troubled relationship with his father's queen, Margareta Eriksdotter. After Sverre's death, Margareta attempted to return to her native Sweden with her daughter by Sverre, Kristina. Håkon's men forcibly
2 CONC  separated her from her daughter, as he wanted to keep her at his court. Subsequently, Margareta seemingly settled with Håkon and went to his court. During Christmas in 1203, Håkon fell ill after a bloodletting, and on 1 January 1
2 CONC 204 he died. His death was suspected as poisoning and his stepmother Margareta was suspected of the crime. In the end she had one of her men undergo a trial by ordeal on her behalf to prove her innocence, but the man was badly bur
2 CONC ned. This was taken as proof of her guilt, and she had to flee back to Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon Sverresson was not married, and at his death no heirs were known. He was therefore succeeded by his 4-year-old nephew Guttorm Sigurdsson. But after his death a woman, Inga of Varteig, whom Håkon had taken as a concubine for 
2 CONC a time in 1203, appeared at the Birkebeiner court with an infant son who she claimed was Håkon's son. The child had been born in present-day Østfold after the death of the putative father. The boy, named Håkon after his father, la
2 CONC ter became king Håkon IV. In the summer of 1218, Inga underwent a successful trial by ordeal (bore iron) in Bergen to show the paternity of her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Håkon's brief reign, he managed to release Norway from the church's interdict, and end the civil wars, at least for a time. Whether the peace would have lasted if he had been allowed to live is impossible to say. As it turn
2 CONC ed out, his early death sparked a renewal of the fighting, as the bagler pretender Erling Steinvegg in a matter of months gathered an army and went to Norway to claim the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon was buried in the old cathedral in Bergen. The cathedral was demolished in 1531, and the site is today marked by a memorial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our main sources to Håkon's life are Sverris saga and the Bagler sagas, both written shortly after the events, in the 13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Håkon var Norges konge fra 9. mars 1202, til sin død 1. januar 1204. Han ble aldri gift, men fikk ett barn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historisk kalt Håkon III av Norge.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Håkon Sverresson, född omkring 1177, död 1 januari 1204, var kung av Norge 1202-1204. Håkon Sverresson var frilloson till Sverre Sigurdsson av Norge och blev kung efter dennes död. Under den korta tid han satt på tronen lyckades h
2 CONC an skapa fred mellan konung och biskopar. Ryktena sade att hans styvmor, Margareta Eriksdotter, skulle ha förgiftat honom. Håkon fick med frillan Inga från Varteig (omkring 1185-1234) den postumt födde sonen kung Håkon Håkonsson a
2 CONC v Norge (1204-1263).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Konge 1202 - 1204
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT BIOGRAFI:
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon III Sverresson (født 1182 i Nidaros (?), død 1. januar 1204 i Bergen) var konge av Norge fra 1202 til 1204 etter at faren kong Sverre Sigurdsson døde den 9. mars 1202.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon Sverresson var således birkebeinenes kongsemne. Håkon var den andre uekte sønnen til kong Sverre. Moren var Astrid Roesdatter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon ble utpekt til konge på et hirdstemne i Nidaros i 1202 og hyllet på Øyratinget seinere på våren. Håkon ble et samlingspunkt som dempet den uforsonlige striden som kretset rundt kong Sverres personlighet. Han kallet biskopene
2 CONC  hjem og fikk forlik med dem og baglerne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon døde brått i 1204, det ble påstått at dødsfallet var mistenkelig og muligens ble han forgiftet av enkedronning Margareta.
2 CONT
2 CONT Håkon Sverresson ble ikke gift, men ifølge sagaen avlet han en sønn med den vakre Inga fra Varteig. Den seinere kong Håkon IV Håkonsson ble født noen få måneder etter at Håkon Sverresson døde. For å fjerne all tvil om slektskapet 
2 CONC lot kongen og hans menn Inga kongsmor bære jernbyrd i Bergen sommeren 1218.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT konge av Norge fra 1202 til 1204 etter at faren kong Sverre Sigurdsson døde den 9. mars 1202. Håkon Sverresson var således birkebeinenes kongsemne. Håkon var den andre uekte sønnen til kong Sverre.
