1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00020605&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, Maid of Norway From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (Gaelic: Mairead or Maighread) (early 1283–September/October 1290), usually known as the Maid of Norway (Norwegian: Jomfruen av Norge, literally The Virgin of Norway), sometimes known as Margaret of Scotland (Margrete av 
2 CONC Skottland), was a Norwegian–Scottish princess who is widely considered to have been Queen of Scots from 1286 until her death, although this is disputed (see below). Her death sparked off the disputed succession which led to the Wa
2 CONC rs of Scottish Independence. She was the daughter of King Eirik II of Norway and Margaret, daughter of King Alexander III of Scotland. Margaret was born in 1283, most likely in early April; it is likely that her mother died at her
2 CONC  birth, but the date of that death is uncertain. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Background
2 CONT
2 CONT When the treaty arranging the marriage of Margaret and Eirik was signed at Roxburgh on 25 July 1281, Alexander III's younger son David had already died in June of 1281, leaving the King of Scots with only one legitimate son, Alexa
2 CONC nder. Consequently, the treaty included a provision for the children of Margaret and Eirik to succeed to the kingdom of the Scots: If it happens that the king of Scotland dies without a lawful son, and any of his sons does not lea
2 CONC ve lawful issue [not sons] and Margaret has children [not sons] by the king of Norway, she and her children shall succeed to the king of Scotland ... or she, even if she is without children, according to Scottish law and custom.[2
2 CONC ] Alexander III made similar provisions when arranging the marriage of his son Alexander to Margaret, daughter of Guy de Dampierre, Count of Flanders, probably also in 1281. The treaty arranging the marriage, signed in December 12
2 CONC 81, included a lengthy and complex document setting out the customs and usages which determined the succession. As well as general statement of principles, the annex includes specific examples of the rights of "A and M" and their 
2 CONC children in particular cases. The document, while confusing in places, appears to favour primogeniture for male heirs, or their descendants, and proximity of blood for female heirs and their descendants.[3] When Prince Alexander d
2 CONC ied in 28 January 1284, leaving only the king's granddaughter Margaret living out of his descendants, Alexander III summoned all thirteen Earls of Scotland, twenty-four barons and the heads of the three main Gaelic kindreds of the
2 CONC  West, Alexander of Argyll, Aonghas Mór of Islay and Alan MacRuari of Garmoran. Done at Scone on 5 February 1284, the signatories agreed to recognise Margaret as "domina and right heir" if neither Alexander had left no posthumous 
2 CONC child and the king had left no children at the time of his death. However, it is unlikely that this was intended to allow Margaret to rule alone as Queen regnant, but rather jointly with her future spouse, whoever he might be.[4] 
2 CONC While unexceptional in the circumstances, this would appear to show that Alexander III had decided on remarriage. He did remarry, to Yolande de Dreux, but died on 19 March 1286. [edit]Lady and Right Heir of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT After King Alexander was buried at Dunfermline Abbey on 29 March 1286, the magnates and clerics of the realm assembled at Scone in parliament to select the Guardians of Scotland who would keep the kingdom for the right heir. At th
2 CONC is time it was thought that Queen Yolande was pregnant, so that Margaret was not yet the obvious successor. It is uncertain what happened to Yolande's child; most likely she had a miscarriage, although other accounts say that her 
2 CONC child was still-born at Clackmannan on Saint Catherine's day (25 November 1286) with the Guardians in attendance to witness the event,[5] just possibly she had a false pregnancy, and there was even one dubious English claim that s
2 CONC he was faking pregnancy.[6] This, according to the oaths taken, made Margaret the heir at three years of age, but within weeks Robert Bruce, 5th Lord of Annandale and his son Robert, Earl of Carrick — the grandfather and father of
2 CONC  the future King Robert Bruce — had raised a rebellion in the south-west, seizing royal castles. This rebellion was soon suppressed, and a Norwegian ambassador came to Scotland in the winter of 1286-1287 to argue Margaret's cause.
2 CONC  Nothing came of this, and until 1289 the Guardians maintained the peace in Scotland between the competing claims of Margaret, Robert Bruce and John Balliol. Far from the Scots displaying any desire to bring Margaret to Scotland, 
2 CONC it was Margaret's father Eric who raised the question again. Eric sent official ambassadors to Edward I of England, then in Gascony, in May of 1289, with papers referring to Margaret as "Queen". Negotiations from this time onwards
2 CONC  were between Edward, who returned to England later in the year, and Eric, and excluded the Scots until Edward met with Robert Bruce and some of the Guardians at Salisbury in October of 1289. The Scots were in a weak position sinc
2 CONC e Edward and Eric could arrange Margaret's marriage to the future Edward II of England, or some other if they chose, without reference to the Guardians. Accordingly the Guardians signed the Treaty of Salisbury, which agreed that M
2 CONC argaret would be sent to Scotland before 1 November 1290, and that any agreement on her future marriage would be deferred until she was in Scotland.[7] That marriage of Edward, Prince of Wales, was in King Edward's mind is clear f
2 CONC rom the fact that a papal dispensation was received from Pope Nicholas IV ten days after the treaty was signed. Sometimes thought to show bad faith on Edward's part, the Papal Bull did not contract a marriage, only permit one shou
2 CONC ld the Scots later agree to it. Edward, like Eric, was now writing of Queen Margaret, anticipating her inauguration and the subsequent marriage to his son.[8] Edward and the Guardians continued their negotiations, based on the col
2 CONC lective assumption that Margaret would be Queen and Edward of Wales King, but all these plans, and those of King Alexander, were brought to nothing by the death of Margaret in the Orkney Islands in late September or early October 
2 CONC of 1290 while voyaging to Scotland. Her remains were taken to Bergen and buried beside her mother in the stone wall, on the north side of the choir, in Christ's Kirk at Bergen. Although derived from a text written more than a cent
2 CONC ury later, it is thought by some historians that the earliest Middle English verse written in Scotland dates from this time: Quhen Alexander our kynge was dede, That Scotland lede in lauche and le, Away was sons of alle and brede,
2 CONC  Of wyne and wax, of gamyn and gle. Our gold was changit into lede. Christ, born in virgynyte, Succoure Scotland, and ramede, That stade is in perplexite.[9] The ballad Sir Patrick Spens has sometimes been supposed to be connected
2 CONC  to Margaret's ill-fated voyage. Some years later a woman appeared claiming to be her, the False Margaret, who was executed by Haakon V, King Eric's brother and successor, in 1301. [edit]Was she queen?
2 CONT
2 CONT As Margaret was never crowned or otherwise inaugurated, and never set foot on what was then Scots soil during her lifetime, there is some doubt about whether she should be regarded as a Queen of Scots. This could ultimately be a m
2 CONC atter of interpretation. Most lists of the monarchs of Scotland do include her, but a few do not. Some contemporary documents, including the Treaty of Salisbury (see above) did describe her as "queen", but it has been argued that 
2 CONC she should not properly be considered Queen regnant.[10] Part of the problem here is the lack of a clear historical precedent. In the whole of Scotland's history as a fully separate country before the Union of the Crowns in 1603 t
2 CONC here was only one occasion when a similar situation arose i.e. on the death of the monarch the heir was outside the country and not available to be crowned more or less immediately. This was when, on the death of Robert III in 140
2 CONC 6, his heir, who became James I, was a prisoner in England. James was eventually released and crowned in 1424. In the intervening period official documents simply referred to him as the "heir", and the Regent Albany issued coins i
2 CONC n his own name. Nevertheless, James's reign is now usually considered to start in 1406, not 1424. [edit]Margaret in popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT Hendry, Frances Mary, Quest for a Maid. Farrar, Strauss & Giroux, 1988. ISBN 0-374-46155-4 [edit]Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Duncan, p. 166. The most probable date for her mother's death is 9 April 1283 as given in the Gesta Annalia, but the Chronicle of Lanercost gives 27-28 February. ^ Duncan, p. 166, citing Acts of the Parliament of Scotland, volum
2 CONC e I, 422b. ^ Duncan, pp.166–169. ^ Macdougall, pp. 12–13; Duncan, pp 169–171. ^ Duncan, p. 178. ^ Traquair ^ Oram, Canmore Kings, p. 109; Duncan, pp 179–183. ^ Duncan, pp. 182–183. ^ Duncan, p. 175; Crawford & Imlah, p. 42. ^ Dunc
2 CONC an, pp.182–182; Oram, Canmore Kings, p. 107. The Cambridge Medieval History says the "Regents" declared her queen, VII, 562.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford, Robert & Mick Imlach, The New Penguin Book of Scottish Verse. Penguin, London, 2001. ISBN 0-14-058711-X Duncan, A.A.M., The Kingship of the Scots 842–1292: Succession and Independence. Edinburgh University Press, Edinbur
2 CONC gh, 2002. ISBN 0-7486-1626-8 Macdougall, Norman, "L'Écosse à la fin du XIIIe sieclè: un royaume menacé" in James Laidlaw (ed.) The Auld Alliance: France and Scotland over 700 Years. Edinburgh University, Edinburgh, 1999. ISBN 0-95
2 CONC 34945-0-0 Oram, Richard (with Michael Penman), The Canmore Kings: Kings and Queens of the Scots, 1040–1290. Tempus, Stroud, 2002. ISBN 0-7524-2325-8 Traquair, Peter Freedom's Sword
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The infant Margaret was proclaimed Queen upon her grandfathers death & her marriage was arranged to Edward. Margaret sailed to take up her position as Queen of Scotland in Sept. 1290, but was fated never to arrive in her new kingd
2 CONC om. Tempestuous Autumnal gales buffeted her ship & she fell sick & died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: The book, 'Kings & Queens of Great Britain' The book, 'Secrets of the Royals' The book, 'The Oxford History of the British Monarchy' The book, 'Scotland, A Concise History' The book, 'Now I Remember' The book, 'They Came 
2 CONC With The Conqueror' Funk & Wagnalls New Encyclopedia
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Läs om flickans öde: http://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000000032750484?photo_id=6000000003590554765&position=0
2 CONT
2 CONT Digitalarkivet | Om Regesta Norvegica | Navne- og sakregister | Søk i Regesta Norvegica | Hjelp | Kontakt  
2 CONT
2 CONT Regesta Norvegica 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nr: 580 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Dato: 1290 juli 18 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sted: Brigham 
2 CONT
2 CONT Regest: Ekteskapstraktat1 mellom prins Edvard av England og jomfru Margareta, dotter til kong Eirik Magnusson av Norge, Skottlands arving og dronning. Traktaten er inngått mellom kong Edvard I av England gjennom 6 sendemenn og ful
2 CONC lmektigar, som også kunngjer traktaten, på den eine sida, og kongeriket Skottlands regentar, andre geistlege og verdslege herrar og heile rikets allmuge på den andre: Dersom ikkje Edvard og Margareta eller ein av dei let etter seg
2 CONC  barn, skal kongeriket Skottland falle tilbake til næraste arvingar; fritt, udelt og utan noen underkasting, slik at Englands konge, hans arvingar eller andre ikkje skal tene eller tape på det. Margareta vil få jordegods i England
2 CONC  som morgongåve, og det blir gitt føresegner om nytt rikssegl når ho kjem til Skottland, om oppbevaring av dokument som vedkjem kongen og kongedømet før og etter ho har fått barn, og likeeins føresegner som sikrar kongedømets mate
2 CONC rielle interesser til riket har fått ein arving. Traktaten skal ratifiserast med kong Edvards eid (jfr nr 595 ), og garanterast av 14 geistlege og verdslege herrar. Under trugsmål om straffebot på 100.000 pund sterling til den rom
2 CONC erske kyrkja, skal kongen forplikte seg til å gjenopprette det skotske kongedømet i samsvar med traktaten og innan eitt år etter festarmålet syte for at traktaten er godkjend av paven.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besegla av utf.
2 CONT
2 CONT b.2, s.219   Kopi (lat perg): PRO-Registrum Munimentorum lib A fol 150 ; PRO-RotLittPat 18 Edward I, membr 9.  Trykt: RymerFœdera I:2 s 735; StevensonDocuments I s 164 ; DN XIX nr 355. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1  tilbakeTraktaten inneheld også detaljerte føresegner som slår fast Skottlands frie og uavhengige stilling overfor England. Desse føresegnene er ikkje refererte i regestteksten.
