2 PLAC Søndre Nes, Nes Parish, Sauherad, Telemark
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} <br/>
2 CONT The tradition is that she was a daughter of Ivar Ogmundsson Rova (c1280-1350) of the noble  Hestbø Family of Finnøy, Rogaland; known as the Hestbø Knights, a family of royal descent. Ivar  served as a knight, King’s Councilor, vic
2 CONC eroy and governor, first of Tønsberg, later of Skien. He  died in the Black Death. The tradition is based on this family’s ownership to the Søndre Nes  Farm, which Cecilia took over. If she was his daughter, she must have been bor
2 CONC n out of wedlock.  Even this is, however, problematic due to several inheritance settlements in the Hestbø Family,  where her legitimate children and successors, despite her own illegitimate birth, should have  had the right of in
2 CONC heritance if she really was his daughter. Hence, most modern genealogist  believe that it’s unlikely that she was his daughter. Søndre Nes might have belonged to the  Hestbø Family for several generations and have been among the r
2 CONC oyal farms of the petty Kingdom of Grenland in the times before Norway was united to a single kingdom. There is a local  tradition that this farm a millennium ago belonged to the father of King Olav II (St. Olaf), and  this is wh
2 CONC ere Olav was born.
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT <br/><br/>
2 CONT <br/>Sauherad III p. 794-795 and 800
