2 CONT https://nbl.snl.no/Margrete_Ingesdatter_%E2%80%9CFredkolla%E2%80%9D
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Margaret Fredkulla==
2 CONT *Fredkulla meaning peacegirl, her marriage to king Magnus of Norway was arranged in order to ensure peace with Norway.
2 CONT *Born Princess of Sweden
2 CONT *By marriage 1101 to King Magnus III, Queen Consort of Norway. (the marriage was childless). 
2 CONT *By marriage 1105 to King Niels I, Queen Consort of Denmark
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Margaret was born a royal princess as one of four children of King Inge the Elder of Sweden and Queen Helena. The exact year of birth and place of birth is not recorded.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1101, she was married to King Magnus of Norway. The marriage had been arranged as a part of the peace treaty between Sweden and Norway. She was often referred to as Margaret Fredkulla (Margaret the Maiden of Peace). She brought
2 CONC  with her large fiefs and areas in Sweden as her dowry, probably in Västergötland. In 1103, she was made widow after two years of marriage, and soon left Norway. The marriage was childless. Her departure from Norway was seen as an
2 CONC  insult by the Norwegians who expected her to stay, and she was accused of having stolen the holy relics of Saint Olav.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1105, she married King Niels of Denmark. Niels was made king in 1104, but he was described as a passive monarch who lacked the capacity to rule and who left the affairs of the state to his queen. With his blessing, Margaret bec
2 CONC ame the de facto Queen regnant of Denmark. She is described as a wise ruler, and the relationship between Denmark and her birth country Sweden was very peaceful during her time as queen. It was said that: Styrelsen beroede for stø
2 CONC rstedelen paa den ædle dronning Margrete, saa at fremmede sagde, at Danmarks styrelse laa i kvindehaand (English: "The rule was so much dependant on the noble Queen Margaret, that foreigners remarked that the rule of Denmark lay i
2 CONC n a woman's hand"). She minted her own coins, something unique for a queen consort of this time. The Danish coins printed during this period bears the inscription: Margareta-Nicalas ("Margaret-Niels").[3] In 1114, Margaret was sen
2 CONC t a letter by Thibaud d'Étampes (Theobaldus Stampensis) thanking her for a liberality to the Church of Caen.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Margaret had two children with King Niels: Inge Nielsen (died as a child) Magnus Nielsen (born about 1106)
2 CONT
2 CONT After her death in 1130, King Niels married Queen dowager Ulvhild of Sweden. Margaret's lands in Sweden became a base for her son, Magnus when he claimed the throne of Sweden through her. When Margaret's first cousin King Inge the
2 CONC  Younger died, Magnus claimed the throne as the eldest grandson of King Inge the Elder and reigned as King Magnus I of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT References[edit] 1.Jump up ^ Margrete Ingesdatter “Fredkolla” (Store norske leksikon) 2.Jump up ^ Margrete Fredkulla (Dansk Kvindehistorie) 3.Jump up ^ Margareta Fredkulla (Ingesdotter) 4.Jump up ^ Bernard Gineste, "Thibaud d'Étam
2 CONC pes", in Cahiers d'Étampes-Histoire 10 (2009), pp. 43–58, showing that this letter was not sent to Margaret of Scotland, dead in 1089, but to Margaret Fredkulla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other sources[edit] Harrison, Dick Gud vill det – Nordiska korsfarare under medeltiden (2005) Nanna Damsholt Kvindebilledet i dansk højmiddelalder (1985)
2 CONT
2 CONT External links[edit] Margareta Fredkulla
2 CONT
