1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir William Sinclair or Saint Clair''' (d. 1330), of Roslin, friend of Robert Bruce, was the son of Sir Henry Sinclair of Roslin [see under Sinclair, Sir William, (fl. 1266–1303)] by Alicia de Fenton. According 
2 CONC to Father Hay, he received from Robert the Bruce the grant of Pentland Moor in free forestry and the office of great master-hunter of Scotland on account of the prowess of his two dogs, ‘Help’ and ‘Hold,’ in capturing a fleet whit
2 CONC e deer which repeatedly baulked the efforts of Bruce's hounds (Genealogy of the Sinclairs, p. 16). He was one of the knights chosen to accompany Sir James Douglas (1286?–1330) [q. v.] to the Holy Land with the heart of Bruce; and,
2 CONC  in view of the service which he was expected to render him, received from Bruce a pension of 40l. (Exchequer Rolls, i. 209). He was slain, along with Douglas, on the plains of Andalusia by the Saracens on 25 Aug. 1330 (Wyntoun, C
2 CONC hronicle). By his wife Isabel—sometimes surnamed Sperra—daughter of Malise, earl of Strathearn*, he had three sons and a daughter: Sir Henry Sinclair, earl or prince of Orkney [q. v.], William, David, and Margaret, who married fir
2 CONC st, Thomas, second earl of Angus, and secondly, Sir William Sinclair of Herdmanston. [Wyntoun's Chronicle; Exchequer Rolls of Scotland, vol. i.; Hay's Genealogy of the Sinclairs of Roslin.] T. F. H.** [Ref: DNB, Editor, Sidney Lee
2 CONC , MacMillan Co, London & Smith, Elder & Co., NY, 1909, vol. xviii, p. 308]
2 CONT
2 CONT ''* SP under "Ancient Earls of Strathearn" gives no such dau. of any of the Earls of Strathearn named Malise & in the article on William under "Sinclair, Earl of Orkney" (see below) does not identify his wife...CH''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''** Thomas Finlayson Henderson, author of this article.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William St. Clair [son of Henry St. Clair of Roslin & Alice de Fenton] had a grant of a pension of £40 in anticipation of his services in the Holy Land 1329,(14-566) being one of the knights chosen to accompany Sir James Dougl
2 CONC as to Palestine with the heart of Bruce, but was slain in Andalusia by the Saracens 25 August 1330.(1-567) He was the father of:-
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. William, next mentioned.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Margaret, married, first, dispensation dated 3 June 1353,(2-567) to Thomas, Earl of Angus, who died in 1361. They were said to be related in the fourth degree. She married, secondly, Sir John Sinclaire of Hermandston.(3-567) Sh
2 CONC e had a pension from King David II. from the lands of Tollie.(4-567)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Thomas, Bailie of Orkney for the King of Norway; a witness to a resignation by Bernard de Rowie to Hugh de Ross of the lands of Fouleroule in Aberdeenshire on 20 January 1364,(5-567) and died before 1 November 1371, leaving a s
2 CONC on:- Alexander, who attested an instrument at Kirkwall in 1364, had a charter from King David in confirmation of a charter by Hugh Ross of Philorth to him of the lands of Estertyre 1 November 1369,(6-567) and of one by William, Ea
2 CONC rl of Ross of the lands of Bray, in the sherffdom of Inverness, on 1 November 1369.(7-567)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. John, a witness in 1367.
2 CONT
2 CONT (14-566) _Exch. Rolls_, i., 209. (1-567) Dalrymple's _Annals_, ii. 131. (2-567) _Cal. of Papal Reg. Letters_, iii. 512. (3-567) Robertson's _Index_, 62, 25. (4-567) _Reg. Mag. Sig._, fol. 29, No. 59. (5-567) _Reg. Aberdon._, i. 10
2 CONC 6. (6-567_Reg. Mag. Sig._, fol. vol. 76, No. 273. (7-567) _Ibid._, 76, No. 274. [Ref: SP VI:566-7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Curt Hofemann
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * ''Burke's Peerage and Baronetage "A Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Peerage and Baronetage"'' by Sir Bernard Burke, 92nd edition. Published by Burke's Peerage Ltd in 1934; 106th edition, Charles Mosley Editor-in-Chief, 
2 CONC 1999.
2 CONT --------------------
