1 NAME Margaret /Sinclair/
2 GIVN Margaret
2 SURN Sinclair
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE BEF 1406
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT (Probably) Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Lady-Margaret-Sinclair/6000000024572304607 who married https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-James-Leask/6000000024572701051?through=6000000024572304607
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From
2 CONT 34 RECORDS OF THE EARLDOM OF ORKNEY
2 CONT XVI
2 CONT Passport or Testimonial given to James of Cragy by
2 CONT the Lawman of Orkney, canons of St. Magnus, and
2 CONT citizens of Kirkwall.
2 CONT Original in Gen. Reg. House. Printed : Spalding Mine., vol. v. ;
2 CONT 0. L. Records, No. 20.
2 CONT Nov. 10th, 1422. Kirkwall.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://deriv.nls.uk/dcn17/1276/7541/127675415.17.jpg 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://digital.nls.uk/scottish-history-society-publications/browse/archive/127675413?mode=transcription
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT James of Cragy, Laird of Hupe, was married to the eldest daughter of Lord Henry Sinclair and his lawful wife Margaret, daughter of the late Earl of Strathearn. A testimonial (or passport) reads,
2 CONT
2 CONT “James Cragy of Hupe, Goodman 1424, and Hirdman of the King of Norway, 1422, married Margaret, Eldest daughter of Henry St Clair before 1422. Testimonial given to James of Cragy by Lawman of Orkney, Canons of St Magnus, and citize
2 CONC ns of Kirkwall, November 10th, 1422.
2 CONT
2 CONT William son of Thurgys (Thorgil), Lawman of Orkney, Nicolas of Anyud, and Lawrence of Turay, Canons of St Magnus Church, John Magnusson, William of Erwin, Peter of Paplay, Walter Andresson, citizens of Kirkwall, testify that James
2 CONC  of Cragy, dominus of Hupe, the bearer of these presents is a liegeman of the King of Norway residing in Orkney and that he is married to Margaret, lawful daughter of Elizabeth of Strathearne and Lord Henry St Clair late Earl of 0
2 CONC rkney ( Elizabeth being lawful daughter of the late Lord Malise of Strathearne, Earl of Orkney. The lawful birth of and the merits of James of Cragy are set forth, and it is particularly mentioned that he was the firmest supporter
2 CONC  of the late Bishop of Orkney, John of Colchester, and endured many troubles through the adversaries of the said Bishop. The seals of the granters of this testimonial are appended at Kirkwall, November 10th, 1422.
2 CONT
2 CONT Endorsement: Ane testimoniall annentes the auld privilege of the Cragys, aviss har (rest illegible.)”
2 CONT
2 CONT - https://rousayremembered.com/craigie-family-history/
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Craigie, W.T.  “Some History of the Craigie Family” (11th March 1980) < [https://rousayremembered.com/craigie-family-history/ link] >
2 CONT
2 CONT The Orkney Craigies appear to be descended from James Cragy of Hupe and from their mother’s side, descended from the Norse Earls of Orkney and the Kings of Norway. It has also been surmised that James’s wife was also descended fro
2 CONC m the Stuart and Bruce royal lineage through David, Earl of Strathearn, who was the legitimate oldest son of King Robert II and Euphemia Ross. It appears more likely that James of Hupe’s bride was descended from the previous Strat
2 CONC hearn line which was extinguished when the Earl of Strathearn was killed at the Battle of Halidon Hill in 1333 [part of the Second War of Scottish Independence, near Berwick-upon-Tweed.] This Earldom reverted to the Crown and was 
2 CONC given to the aforementioned David. …
2 CONT
2 CONT No firm date can be traced of when the first Craigies went to Orkney. The first date I can trace is contained in a document dated 1418, which refers to the “Auld Privilege of the Craigies.” Another refers to the Craigie brethren. 
2 CONC What took them to Orkney, and when? One can only guess. Henry Sinclair, Lord of Roslin, near Edinburgh, married the sole heiress to the Orkney Earldom, and became Earl of Orkney in 1379. He would need knights to assist him in the 
2 CONC administration of the Islands (which were under the crown of Norway). Maybe, John de Cragy of Merchiston had met with hard times! He sold his lands! Manybe he had many sons to provide for, and younger sons had to seek their own fo
2 CONC rtunes. Neighbour Sinclair of Roslin may have provided the answer, or some of it! The Craigie brethren were settled with lands of Pow, and others, by 14th February, 1418, and by 1422 James of Cragy, Laird of Hupe, was married to t
2 CONC he eldest daughter of Lord Henry Sinclair and his lawful wife Margaret, daughter of the late Earl of Strathearn. A testimonial (or passport) reads,
2 CONT
2 CONT >“''James Cragy of Hupe, Goodman 1424, and Hirdman of the King of Norway, 1422, married Margaret, '''Eldest daughter of Henry St Clair''' before 1422. Testimonial given to James of Cragy by Lawman of Orkney, Canons of St Magnus, a
2 CONC nd citizens of Kirkwall, November 10th, 1422.''
2 CONT
2 CONT (But see apparent clerical error for her parentage in the Barbara Crawford article, below.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The testimonial was probably written to enable James to travel to England. Earl Henry St Clair was in command of the expedition which set out to take the young Prince James (afterwards King James I) to France. Their ship was captu
2 CONC red by the English off Flamborough Head and the whole party was kept prisoner by the English king for many years. During this time there were many comings and goings between Scotland and England, hostages exchanged, and negotiatio
2 CONC ns for release made. James Cragy as kinsman of the Orkney Earl would almost certainly be involved. Beyond that he would have a leading role in governing the Islands during the absence of the Earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT In due course, James’s eldest son John was appointed Lawman of Orkney, a position he held from 1455 to 1480. John’s son William succeeded as Lawman and held the position from 1480 to 1495, and William’s son John became Lawman from
2 CONC  1495 to 1509.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Lawman was the President of the “Thing,” (Norse-type Parliament). He was the Keeper and Expounder of the Law book and Chief Judge of Orkney. The Lawman was, therefore, the chief magistrate under Norwegian law. An important par
2 CONC t of his duty was to uphold the rights of the citizens under Udal Law and to keep the Law book. It has been said that the Scots Lawmen did not understand properly their duties and so the Norse “liberties,” were gradually eroded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Udal Law when a landowner died his land was divided between all his children. Thus, in time, the land holdings tended to become smaller and smaller. In practice, one of the heirs bought out the other shares and so preserved 
2 CONC the estate. The Lawman and his magistrates in council confirmed the settlement and registered it in the Law book.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1468 the Princess of Norway (and Denmark) married King James III of Scotland. Part of her dowry was the Orkney Isles and so, for the first time Orkney became part of the Scottish realm. But it was confirmed that the old Norse L
2 CONC aw and privileges would be respected. In 1472 James III decreed that the fee of the Lawman was to be paid only on the authority of the king. In 1470 he contracted an agreement from the Earl of Orkney that the crown take over the E
2 CONC arldom. Lord Sinclair was given lands in Fife, at Ravenscraig, in compensation.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Craigie continued to preside over “The Thing,” (the Orkney Parliament in which the king, earl, bishop, Odallers, and Odal born, were equals. In 1509 the office of Lawman passed out of the Craigie family. By that time, John’s 
2 CONC son Magnus had become Laird of the whole Island of Rousay. In 1503 Sir Thomas Craigie and John Craigie were registered as Tacksmen of the lands of Quhan, Rousay, and James Craigie and his wife were Tacksmen of the Isle of Wyre. In
2 CONC  1564 Sir Nicol Craigie was Vicar of Holm. In 1580 William Craigie of Pabdale (Kirkwall) was Provost of Kirkwall and Commissioner to Parliament. Magnus Craigie was a Kirkwall councillor in 1580 and another Magnus was a councillor 
2 CONC in 1615. (He officiated at several of the trials of witches.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/568/mode/1up '''The Scots Peerage'''], Vol. 6, “Sinclair, Earl of Orkney” (page 568-570) I. HENRY ST. CLAIR … was formally invested EARL OF 
2 CONC ORKNEY and LORD OF ZETLAND, under certain onerous restrictions …. He married Jean, daughter of Sir Walter Halyburton of Dirleton, and had issue : —  … 5. '''Margaret''', married to James of Cragy, Laird of Hupe in Orkney. Her husb
2 CONC and had a passport by the Lawman of Orkney and Canons of the Chapter of St. Magnus on 10 November 1422 9 in which she is erroneously called daughter (instead of granddaughter) of Henry Sinclair, Lord of Orkney and his wife [https:
2 CONC //www.geni.com/people/Lady-Isabella-de-Strathearn-Countess-of-Caithness/6000000006444798390?through=6000000002240813831 Elizabeth], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Malise-8th-Earl-of-Strathearn/6000000000701222268?through
2 CONC =6000000006444798390 Malise of Strathern.].  ##Comment: Elizabeth (or Isabella) of Strathearn was the wife of https://www.geni.com/people/William-Sinclair-of-Roslin/4226922301370045350?through=6000000006444798390, the mother of ht
2 CONC tps://www.geni.com/people/Henry-Sinclair-1st-Earl-of-Orkney/6000000002240813831?through=4226922301370045350.  TSP shows Margaret as Henry’s daughter by his wife Jean.
2 CONT # Apparently Burke’s, as shown at http://thepeerage.com/p49548.htm#i495478, shows her without mother defined and as the wife of James Leask.
2 CONT # Craigie, W.T.  “Some History of the Craigie Family” (11th March 1980) < [https://rousayremembered.com/craigie-family-history/ link] >
2 CONT # WikiTree contributors, "Margaret Sinclair (abt.1360-)," WikiTree: The Free Family Tree, (https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Sinclair-3401 : accessed 10 July 2025). cites 
2 CONT ## “The Scottish Nation - Craigie” < [https://www.electricscotland.com/history/nation/craigie2.htm link] > In Orkney there was a family of Craigie from an early period. The first of whom any notice has been found in James of Craig
2 CONC y, dominus de Hupe, who married a daughter of Henry Sinclair, earl of Orkney. He is supposed to have accompanied that noble to Orkney, when he received an investiture of the earldom from the king of Denmark, in 1379, and was proba
2 CONC bly a son of John de Craigie and Margaret de Mondofe. Of that marriage, besides a daughter, there were at least 2 sons.
2 CONT # Crawford, Barbara E. ''The Earls of Orkney-Caithness and their relations with Norway and Scotland, 1158-1470''. Ph.D. dissertation, St. Andrews University (1971). Accessed 10 July 2025.  < [https://research-repository.st-andrews
2 CONC .ac.uk/bitstream/handle/10023/2723/BarbaraECrawfordPhDThesis.pdf?sequence=7&isAllowed=y PDF] > p. 225, p. 54-55. There is however one important conflicting piece of evidence which concerns the Sinclairs. It is stated quite clearly
2 CONC  in the quite clearly in the Genealogy that one of Malise's daughters was married to Sir William Sinclair, and their son Henry was granted the earldom of Orkney by King Hakon5. There exists however a letter written in 1422 by the 
2 CONC Lawman and Canons of St. Magnus in favour of James Cragy, lord of Hupe, which states that he-was married to the daughter of Elizabeth (or Isabelle) of Strathearn (daughter of Malise) and Lord Henry Sinclair, former earl of Orkney.
2 CONC 1. There is no other evidence to support this information, which appears to show that it was the first Sinclair earl who was married to Malise's heiress. It has been suggested that in the 1422 document Henry was a clerical slip fo
2 CONC r William. 2.  This however is unlikely as Henry is called earl of Orkney and it must have been well-known in 1422 that William Sinclair has never been earl through right of his wife.   More likely is a clerical slip which called 
2 CONC Margaret Sinclair daughter of Elizabeth instead of grand-daughter. This is understandable because Elizabeth is not even referred to as ‘quondam’ in 1422, as her son Henry was, which means that she was probably still alive.  Her re
2 CONC lationship with her grand-daughter  was therefore more relevant than the father’s, and her importance is emphasized by her being mentioned first before Earl Henry. The Genealogy’s statement is therefore still accepted as the corre
2 CONC ct one.
2 CONT ## 1. Orkney-Shetland Recs.. p. 41.
2 CONT ## ER. viii. p. 1xxvi.
