1 OCCU Kung på Isle of Man, King of Man & the Isles, Kung över Lewis i Hebriderna, Konge på Isle of Man 1226-1237
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Óláfr Guðrøðarson''', commonly known in English as '''Olaf the Black''', was a mid 13th century sea king who ruled the Isle of Man (Mann) and parts of the Hebrides. 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Wikipedia ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Olaf II "The Black" Gudrödsson''', King of Mann and the Isles
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Óláfr Guðrøðarson''' (IPA: 'oːlaːvr 'guðruðarsson), commonly known in English as '''Olaf the Black''', was a mid 13th century sea-king who ruled the Isle of Man (Mann) and parts of the Hebrides. Óláfr was the son of Guðrøðr Ólá
2 CONC fsson, King of the Isles, King of Dublin, and his wife Finnguala, granddaughter of Muirchertach Mac Lochlainn, High King of Ireland, King of Cenél nEógain. Óláfr was a younger son of his father; his elder brother Rögnvaldr more th
2 CONC an likely had a different mother. According to the Chronicle of Mann, Guðrøðr appointed Óláfr as heir since he had been born "in lawful wedlock". Whether or not this is the case, on Guðrøðr's death in 1187 the Manxmen instead appo
2 CONC inted Rögnvaldr as king, as he was a capable adult and Óláfr was a mere child. Rögnvaldr ruled the Crovan dynasty's island-kingdom for almost 40 years, during which time the half-brothers vied for the kingship.
2 CONT
2 CONT At one point Óláfr, who had been given possession of Lewis, complained to Rögnvaldr that his lands were not enough. Rögnvaldr's response was seize Óláfr and send him to the King of Scots, where he was imprisoned for almost seven y
2 CONC ears. Upon his release, Óláfr undertook a pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela, after which the half-brothers were reconciled and Rögnvaldr had Óláfr married to Lauon, the sister of his own wife. Sometime after 1217 this marriage 
2 CONC was nullified by Reginald, Bishop of the Isles, who may have been an ally of Óláfr against Rögnvaldr. Óláfr then married [http://www.geni.com/people/Christina-MacTaggart/6000000004533877074 Christina], a daughter of the King of Sc
2 CONC ots' protégé Ferchar, Earl of Ross. The chronicle claims that Rögnvaldr's bitter wife tricked their own son, Guðrøðr, into attempting to kill Óláfr; however, Óláfr narrowly escaped with his life and fled to the protection of his f
2 CONC ather-in-law on the mainland. Together with a loyal follower, one Páll Bálkason, Óláfr later defeated Guðrøðr on Skye.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1220s Rögnvaldr formed an alliance with Alan, Lord of Galloway, in an attempt to fend off Óláfr. Rögnvaldr married his daughter to one of Alan's sons, and it has been theorised that this son was intended to inherit the isla
2 CONC nd-kingdom. Rögnvaldr's actions enraged the Manxmen and in 1226 they deposed him in favour of Óláfr. Rögnvaldr was later killed battling Óláfr in 1229.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1230 Óláfr fled to Norway to seek military assistance against Alan and members of Clann Somairle. The Norwegian king's response was to send a fleet into the Isles under the command of Óspakr Ögmundsson, a member of Clann Somair
2 CONC le. Óspakr was slain early in the campaign, after which Óláfr took control of the fleet and secured himself on Mann. The island-kingdom was divided between him and his mutilated nephew Guðrøðr, with the latter ruling the Hebridean
2 CONC  portion and Óláfr ruling Mann itself. Guðrøðr was soon after killed on Lewis, and Óláfr ruled the whole Kingdom of Mann and the Isles peacefully, until his death in 1237. Óláfr's restoration on Mann was seen as a success by the N
2 CONC orwegians, and likely favourably viewed by the Scots as well; since the internal struggle between him and his rivals had been brought to an end. Óláfr was succeeded by his son, Haraldr. In all, three of Óláfr's sons ruled the Crov
2 CONC an dynasty's island-kingdom—the last of which, Magnus Olafsson, was also the last of the dynasty to rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT * English Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olaf_the_Black Olaf the Black]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family ==
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married three times. 
2 CONT
2 CONT > "[Olave the Black] died about 1237, having been thrice married ; first, to a daughter of one of the leading families of Kintyre, by whom he had three sons--Harold, Reginald, and Magnus, all of whom successively reigned as Kings 
2 CONC of Man. . . . Olave the Red [sic] had no issue by his second marriage ; but having married, thirdly, Christina, daughter of Farquhar, Earl of Ross, he had by her three sons--1. Leod, or Loyd, Progenitor of the Macleods. 2. Guin, f
2 CONC rom whom the Clan Gunn of Sutherland and Caithness, and 3. Leandruis, of whom Clan Leandruis, or Gillanders" ([https://books.google.com/books?id=mmZJAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA5&lpg=PA5&dq=leandruis&source=bl&ots=mHdIWTQp3v&sig=GJLDKCUQQTxDTHv
2 CONC KUA67yPNB220&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwim8sa7tsPbAhUzFzQIHdZIAywQ6AEINTAC#v=onepage&q=leandruis&f=false MacKenzie], pp. 4-5).
2 CONT
2 CONT Another source says:
2 CONT
2 CONT > "Some time after his return to Lodhus, Olaf decided to marry again in 1218 to ‘Jauon’ (i.e., Joan) a sister of the Queen of Man. Reginald, the Bishop of the Isles, now took action and convening the Synod, demanded that Olaf must
2 CONC  divorce his wife on the ground that she was cousin german to his first wife. Olaf complained that his first marriage was not confirmed: Bishop Reginald was adamant: Joan had to go. In 1222, Olaf married his third wife, Christina 
2 CONC daughter of Farquhar, Earl of Ross."
2 CONT
2 CONT Married (1) Unknown lady of Kintyre
2 CONT
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Harald-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsson-king-of-Man/6000000002512272355 Harald I] (died 1248)
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Reginald-Olafsson-king-of-Man/6000000017345024955 Ragnvald] (died 1249)
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Magnus-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsson-king-of-Man/6000000006804348076 Magnus] (died 1265)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married (2) Lavon, a cousin of his wife and sister of the unnamed wife of [https://www.geni.com/people/Ragnvald-IV-Gudr%C3%B6dsson-King-of-Mann-and-the-Isles/6000000001496413976 Ragnvald IV Gudrödsson],
2 CONT
2 CONT Married (3) Christine, daughter of Ferquhard, earl of Ross
2 CONT
2 CONT # ''(wrongly attributed)'' [https://www.geni.com/people/Leod-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsson/6000000079107405595 Leod]
2 CONT # ''(wrongly attributed)'' [https://www.geni.com/people/Gunni-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsson/6000000079108358879 Gunni], ancestor of Clan Gunn
2 CONT # ''(wrongly attributed)'' [https://www.geni.com/people/Leanders-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsson/6000000079108367869 Leaundres], ancestor of Clan Leanders or Gillanders
2 CONT # ''(perhaps)'' [https://www.geni.com/people/Godred-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsson/6000000053910720837 Godred] (died 1238)
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf has also been speculated as father:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wife of [https://www.geni.com/people/Leod-1st-Chief-of-Clan-MacLeod/6000000002188078500 Leod] (above)
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/people/%C3%93l%C3%A1fsd%C3%B3ttir-hypothetical/6000000009727709504 Wife of Óttarr Snaekollson]
2 CONT
