2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Gudrod-the-Black-King-of-Man-the-North-Isles/6000000002076169166?through=6000000001744995580 GODRED] (-Isle of St Patrick, Isle of Man 10 Nov 1187, bur Iona).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “
2 CONC Godredum” as the child of “Olavus filius Godredi Crovan” and his wife “Affricam…filiam Fergus de Galwedia”[1295].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “Godred filius eius” returned from Norway after his father was kille
2 CONC d, murdered one of his cousins and blinded the other two, after which he reigned for 33 years[1296].  King of Man.  "Guthredus…rex Insularum" exchanged land "Eschedala" for "ecclesia sancti Olavi et villula…Euastad" with St Bees b
2 CONC y undated charter, witnessed by "…Gillochristo fratre et collactaneo meo…"[1297].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that King Godred was defeated by Somerled Lord of Argyll in 1158 and fled to Norway to seek help[1298].  
2 CONC The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records the death “IV Id Nov” in 1187 of King Godred “in insula Sancti Patricii in Mannia” and his burial the following summer in “insulam…Hy”[1299].  m (1176) [https://www.geni.com/people/Finngua
2 CONC la-mac-Lochlainn/6000000000620695488?through=6000000002076169166  FINGOLA], daughter of --- of Ireland & his wife ---.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that King Godred married “Phingola filia MacLotlen filii Murkartac r
2 CONC egis Hiberniæ”, mother of his son, in a Christian marriage ceremony in 1176[1300].  Godred & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>a)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Olaf-II-the-Black-King-of-Isle-of-Man/1341507?through=6000000000620695488 '''OLAV'''] ([1172/73]-21 May 1237, bur Rushen St Mary).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names Olav as the son 
2 CONC of King Godred and his wife, adding that he was three years old when his parents were married[1301].  King of Man. OLAV, son of GODRED King of Man & his wife Fingola --- ([1172/73]-21 May 1237, bur Rushen St Mary).  The Chronicon 
2 CONC Manniæ et Insularum names Olav as the son of King Godred and his wife, adding that he was three years old when his parents were married[1329].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that the Manxman chose Olav’s older brother 
2 CONC Ragnall as king, against the wishes of their father, adding in a later passage that Ragnall granted the island of Lewes to Olav but that Olav was later imprisoned by William I King of Scotland, was freed by the latter’s son King A
2 CONC lexander I and returned to Lewes[1330].  King of Man.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav recovered Man from his half-brother in 1226[1331].  The History of Olave the Black King of Man records that “Olave son of G
2 CONC odred was…King of Man” in 1229 when he resisted Alan Lord of Galloway[1332].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav kept Man when he agreed to divide his kingdom with his nephew Godred in 1230, the latter ruling in t
2 CONC he Isles[1333].  "Olavus rex Insularum" donated "boves…oves et porcos" to St Bees by undated charter, witnessed by "…Therkillo filio Nigelli…"[1334].  Henry III King of England made a grant of property to "Olaf King of Man and the
2 CONC  Isles" for "his homage and services in guarding at his expense the coast of the English sea towards Ireland and the Isle of Man…" by charter dated 11 Jul 1235[1335].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records the death “XII Kal J
2 CONC un” in 1237 of “Olavus Godredi filius rex Manniæ et Insularum” and his burial “in abbatia Sanctæ Mariæ de Russin”[1336]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>m firstly ---.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav had previously married “consobrinam” of his second wife, which provided the reason for his divorce[1337]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>m secondly JOAN, daughter of ---.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that King Ragnall arranged the marriage of his half-brother Olav to “filiam cujusdam nobilis de Kentyre germanam uxoris suæ, nomine Jauon” from whom he
2 CONC  was later divorced on grounds of consanguinity[1338]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>m thirdly [https://www.geni.com/people/Christina-MacTaggart-Queen-of-Mann-and-the-Isles/6000000004533877074 CHRISTINA], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Fearchar-Mac-an-t-Sagairt-Earl-of-Ross/6000000006724737125?through=
2 CONC 6000000004533877074 FERQUHARD MacTaggart] Earl of Ross & his wife ---.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav of Man married thirdly “Scristinam filiam Ferkar comitis de Ros”[1339]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>Olav & his third wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>1.         [https://www.geni.com/people/no-name/6000000176946244893?through=6000000004533877074 HARALD] ([1222/23]-drowned 1249).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “Haraldus filius eius” succeeded as king on the d
2 CONC eath of Olav in 1237, being 14 years old and reigning for 12 years[1340].  King of Man.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Harald and his wife were drowned while returning to Man from Norway in 1249[1341].  m ([1246/4
2 CONC 7]) as her second husband, CECILIA Haakonsdatter, widow of GREGORIUS Andersson, illegitimate daughter of HAAKON "den Gamle/the Old" King of Norway & his mistress --- (-drowned 1248).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that
2 CONC  Harald King of Man married “rex Norwegiæ…filiam suam”[1342].  The Chronicle of Lanercost records that "Haraldum regem Manniæ" married "Haco rex Nortweyæ…filiam suam" in 1247[1343].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that 
2 CONC Harald and his wife were drowned while returning to Man from Norway in 1249[1344]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>2.         [https://www.geni.com/people/Godred-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsson/6000000053910720837?through=6000000004533877074 GODRED] (-drowned 1237 or after).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “Godredum Holavi filium…puerum” when r
2 CONC ecording that “Loglenum consanguineis suis”, whom his brother had left as regent in Man, fled with him to Wales but were drowned on the way[1345]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>3.         [https://www.geni.com/people/Reginald-Olafsson-king-of-Man/6000000017345024955?through=6000000053910720837 RAGNALD] (-killed Rushen 30 May 1249, bur Rushen St Mary).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “R
2 CONC eginaldus frater eius” succeeded after the death of King Harald in 1249[1346].  King of Man.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that King Ragnall was killed 30 May 1249 “ab Yvaro milite…in prato quodam prope ecclesiam Sanc
2 CONC tæ Trinitatis in Russin” and was buried “in ecclesia Sanctæ Mariæ de Russin”[1347]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>4.         [https://www.geni.com/people/Magnus-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsson-king-of-Man/6000000006804348076?through=6000000017345024955 MAGNUS] (-24 Nov 1265, bur Rushen St Mary).  A charter dated 14 Nov 1251 records that "Magnus Mac Ola
2 CONC ve Duff proposed to raise a force in Ireland to invade the territory of the King of Norway in the Isle of Man" and the order of King Henry III that "no force shall be permitted to leave Ireland for that purpose"[1348].  The Chroni
2 CONC con Manniæ et Insularum records that “Magnus filius Olavi” returned to Man in 1252 and installed as king[1349].  King of Man.  Haco’s Expedition against Scotland names “Magnus King of Man” in 1263[1350].  The Continuator of the Hi
2 CONC story of William of Newburgh records the death in 1265 of “the King of Man” after which the island “became tributary to the king of Scotland, who paid for it yearly a certain sum to the king of Norway [and] kings ceased to reign i
2 CONC n Man”[1351].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records the death “VIII Kal Dec” in 1265 of King Magnus and his burial “in abbatia Sanctæ Mariæ de Russyn”, after which Man was transferred to Alexander III King of Scotland[1352]. 
2 CONC  The Liber Pluscardensis records the death in 1267 of "regulus Manniæ"[1353].  m as her first husband, [https://www.geni.com/people/M%C3%A1ire-de-E%C3%B3gain/6000000006804155423?through=6000000006804348076 MARY of Argyll], daughte
2 CONC r of [https://www.geni.com/people/Ewan-MacDougal-Lord-of-Lorn/6000000000424851399?through=6000000006804155423 EWEN of Argyll] & his wife --- (-[28 Sep 1300/10 Oct 1303], bur London, Grey Friars Church).  The Liber Pluscardensis re
2 CONC cords the marriage of "regulus Manniæ…viduam…filiam domini de Arcadia" and "comes de Strathern Malisius"[1354].  The Extracta ex Cronicis Scocie records the death of "Rex Mannie" and the marriage of "eius relictam, filiam comitis 
2 CONC Ergadie Eugenii" to "Malisius comes de Stratherne", among passages dealing with events in 1268[1355].  The primary source which confirms her name has not yet been identified.   She married secondly ([1268]) as his fourth wife, Mal
2 CONC ise Earl of Strathearn, thirdly (before 5 Apr 1281) Hugh Abernethy of that Ilk, and fourthly (before 10 Apr 1299) as his second wife, William FitzWarin.  "Maria Regina de Man quondam uxor domini Willelmi filii Warini" is named in 
2 CONC the Wardrobe Accounts in Dec 1299[1356].  The deaths of “dñs Willm Fizwarryn Baro et Isabella ux sua quoda Regina Man” are recorded in Grey Friars Church, London[1357].  Magnus & his wife had [one child]:    
