1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olaf_the_Black Extract: There is evidence to suggest that Óláfr might have had a fourth son named Guðrøðr.[49] For example, the chronicle relates how the governor of Mann, described a
2 CONC s a kinsman of Haraldr, fled from the king in 1238 and set sail for Wales, taking with him his foster-son Guðrøðr Óláfsson. When the fleeing ship reached the Welsh coast it was wrecked and, according to the chronicle, Guðrøðr peri
2 CONC shed on board.[44] Furthermore, amongst the names of witnesses within a quitclaim between Llywelyn ap Gruffudd (d. 1282) and Ralph de Mortimer (d. 1246), thought to date to about 1241, there is a certain Guðrøðr who appears in Lat
2 CONC in as Godredo filio regis Mannie ("Guðrøðr, son of the King of Mann").[50][51][note 13] Although the possibility has been raised that the two sources may refer to the same man, there is no further evidence to confirm it.[49]
2 CONT
