2 CONT ALFONSO III
2 CONT
2 CONT , king of Aragón and count of Barcelona
2 CONT
2 CONT 1265–91, king of Aragón and count of Barcelona (1285–91), son and successor of Peter III. He was forced to grant wide privileges to the cortes of the Aragonese nobles. At first he supported the claim to Sicily of his brother James
2 CONC  (later James II of Aragón) against Charles II of Naples. Later, however, he recognized papal suzerainty over Sicily and pressed James to abandon his claim. He also made war on Castile and on his uncle, James I of Majorca. James I
2 CONC I succeeded him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________ 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso III of Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso III (1265 – 18 June 1291), called the Liberal (el Liberal) or the Free (also "the Frank," from el Franc), was the King of Aragon and Count of Barcelona (as Alfons II) from 1285. He conquered the Kingdom of Majorca between 
2 CONC his succession and 1287.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a son of Peter III of Aragon and his Queen consort Constantia of Sicily, daughter and heiress of Manfred of Sicily. His maternal grandmother Beatrice of Savoy was a daughter of Amadeus IV of Savoy and Anne of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after assuming the throne, he conducted a campaign to reincorporate the Balearic Islands into the Kingdom of Aragon - which had been lost due to the division of the kingdom by his grandfather, James I of Aragon. Thus in 1285 
2 CONC he declared war on his uncle, James II of Majorca, and conquered both Majorca (1285) and Ibiza (1286), effectively reassuming suzerainty over the Kingdom of Majorca. He followed this with the conquest of Minorca - until then, an a
2 CONC utonomous Muslim state (Manûrqa) under the Kingdom of Majorca - on 17 January 1287, the anniversary of which now serves as Minorca's national holiday.
2 CONT
2 CONT He initially sought to maintain the Aragonese control over Sicily early in his reign by supporting the claims to island of his brother, James II of Aragon. However, he later pressed his brother to retract the claims and instead su
2 CONC pported claim from the Papal States.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign was marred by a constitutional struggle with the Aragonese nobles, which eventually culminated in the articles of the Union of Aragon - the so called "Magna Carta of Aragon", which devolved several key royal powers into 
2 CONC the hands of lesser nobles. His inability to resist the demands of his nobles was to leave a heritage of disunity in Aragon and further dissent amongst the nobility, who increasingly saw little reason to respect the throne, and br
2 CONC ought the Kingdom of Aragon close to anarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at the age of 27 in 1291, and buried in Barcelona. He bore no children, despite being married on August 15, 1290 to Princess Eleanor of England, daughter of Edward I of England and Princess Eleanor of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dante Alighieri, in the Divine Comedy, recounts that he saw Alfonso's spirit seated outside the gates of Purgatory with the other monarchs whom Dante blamed for the chaotic political state of Europe during the 13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT Alighieri, Dante, Purgatorio, Canto VII, l. 115ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nelson, Lynn. The Chronicle of San Juan De LA Pena: A Fourteenth-Century Official History of the Crown of Aragon (University of Pennsylvania Press, 1991) ISBN 0-8122-1352-1
2 CONT
2 CONT O'Callaghan, Joseph. A History of Medieval Spain (Cornell University Press, 1983) ISBN 0-8014-9264-5
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Afonso III de Aragão, apelidado de o Liberal ou o Franco (1265-1291) foi rei de Aragão como Afonso III, do Valência como Afonso I, Conde de Barcelona como Afonso II entre 1285 e 1291, e rei de Maiorca como Afonso I entre 1286 e 12
2 CONC 91. Monarca de débil carácter que nos seus seis anos de reinado cedeu ante as exigências dos nobres e da curia romana. A conquista de Menorca, a assinatura do Tratado de Tarascón devido aos conflitos derivados da posse da Sicília 
2 CONC e os conflitos com a nobreza aragonesa foram os aspectos mais significativos de seu reinado.
