1 OCCU Count of Toulouse, Duke of Narbonne and Marquis of Provence, Duke of Narbonne, Marquis of Provence
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Raymond VII, Count of Toulouse was born on July 1197 in Saint Gilles, Gard, Languedoc, France to Raymond VI, Count of Toulouse and Joan Plantagenet, Queen of Sicily.  Raymond married (1.) Sancha of Aragon on March 1210/11 and had 
2 CONC a child:  Joan of Toulouse.  They divorced on 1241.  Raymond married (2.) Marguerite de Lusignan on 1243.  They divorced on August 3, 1249.  He passed away on September 27, 1249 and is buried in Fontevraud Abbey, Fontevraud l'Abba
2 CONC ye, Anjou, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond VII, Count of Toulouse is my 26th great uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Raymond VII of Saint-Gilles (July 1197 – 27 September 1249) was Count of Toulouse, Duke of Narbonne and Marquis of Provence from 1222 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond was born in Beaucaire, Gard, the son of Raymond VI of Toulouse and Joan of England. Through his mother, he was a grandson of Henry II of England and a nephew of kings Richard I and John of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond VII married firstly, in March 1211, Sancha of Aragon, the daughter of King Alfonso II of Aragon. They had one daughter, Joan, and were divorced in 1241. He was engaged to Sanchia of Provence, but she married Richard of Cor
2 CONC nwall instead.[1] In 1243 Raymond married Margaret of Lusignan, the daughter of Hugh X of Lusignan and Isabella of Angoulême. They had no children and were divorced on 3 August 1245 after an inquisition found their marriage to be 
2 CONC consanguineous. He then tried to get support of Blanche, Queen mother of France to marry Beatrice of Provence, who had just become Countess of Provence, but Beatrice married Blanche's son Charles instead.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Albigensian Crusade in May 1216, he set out from Marseille and besieged Beaucaire, which he captured on 24 August. He fought to reconquer the county of Toulouse from Simon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester and later Si
2 CONC mon's son Amaury VI of Montfort.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1222. At the moment of his accession, he and the new count of Foix, Roger Bernard II the Great, besieged Carcassonne. On 14 September 1224, the Albigensian Crusaders surrendered and the war came to an en
2 CONC d, each southern lord making peace with the church. However, in 1225, the council of Bourges excommunicated him and launched a crusade against him, the king of France, Louis VIII, called the Lion, wanting to renew the conflict in 
2 CONC order to enforce his royal rights in Languedoc. Roger-Bernard tried to keep the peace, but the king rejected his embassy and the counts of Foix and Toulouse took up arms again. The war was largely a discontinuous series of skirmis
2 CONC hes and, in January 1229, Raymond, defeated, was forced to sign the Treaty of Paris (also known as the "Treaty of Meaux"), by which he ceded the former viscounty of Trencavel to the king and his daughter Joan was forced to marry A
2 CONC lphonse, brother of the new king, Louis the Lion's successor, Louis IX.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Raymond died, Alphonse became count of Toulouse, and after Alphonse's death the county was annexed by France. Raymond VII was buried beside his mother Joan in Fontevrault Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT * 1.  [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=gonefishin&id=I133584]
2 CONT * 2.  [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_VII,_Count_of_Toulouse]
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
