1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''ROBERT [VI] de Brus''' (Jul 1243-c 4 Apr 1304, bur Abbey of Holm Cultram)1295 as Lord of Annandale
2 CONT
2 CONT s/o ROBERT [V] & Isabel de Clare
2 CONT
2 CONT x (Turnberry Castle 1271) MARGARET Ctss of Carrick suo iure, widow of ADAM de Kilconquhar, d & heiress o NEIL Earl of Carri
2 CONT * 1.         ISABEL Bruce (1272-1358) 
2 CONT * 2.         MARY Bruce ([1273]-before 22 Sep 1323)  
2 CONT * 3.   ROBERT [VII] Bruce (Writtle 11 Jul 1274-Cardross Castle, Dumbartonshire 7 Jun 1329, bur Dunfermline Abbey, Fife) 
2 CONT * 4.         EDWARD Bruce (-killed in battle Dundalk 14 Oct 1318) 
2 CONT * 5.         THOMAS Bruce (-beheaded Carlisle Castle 9 Feb 1307) 
2 CONT * 6.         ALEXANDER Bruce (-beheaded Carlisle Castle 9 Feb 1307) 
2 CONT * 7.         CHRISTIAN Bruce (-[1356/27 Jan 1357], bur Dunfermline) 
2 CONT * 8.         MAUD Bruce (-after Sep 1323, bur Fearn) 
2 CONT * 9.         NIGEL [Neil] Bruce (-beheaded Berwick Sep 1306)  
2 CONT * 10.      MARGARET Bruce  
2 CONT * 11.     NN x THOMAS Randolph  
2 CONT
2 CONT xx  (licence 19 Sep 1295, divorced [Oct 1296/May 1299]) MATILDA, widow of PHILIP Burnell, d/o JOHN FitzAlan Lord of Clun and Oswestry &  Isabel de Mortimer (-< [1330]).
2 CONT
2 CONT xxx ELEANOR, d/o  NN (-[13 Apr/8 Sep] or [16 Mar/19 Oct] 1331)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#RobertBrusdied1304
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert de Brus (b July 1243 - d March 1304, 6th Lord of Annandale (dominus vallis Anandie), jure uxoris Earl of Carrick, Lord of Hartness, Writtle and Hatfield Broad Oak (Wretele et Hatfeud Regis), was a cross-border lord[3], 
2 CONC and participant of the Second Barons' War, Welsh Wars, and First War of Scottish Independence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert de Brus, 5th Lord of Annandale''' and '''Isobel de Clare''', daughter of the Earl of Gloucester and Hertford
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: '''Marjorie, Countess of Carrick'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Isabel''', married King Eric II of Norway in 1293, d 1358 in Bergen, Norway. Many sources list a daughter named Isabel who married Sir Thomas Randolph of Strathdon, High Chamberlain of Scotland. We have listed her as a separat
2 CONC e daughter until her parentage can be determined.
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Christina or Christian''' b c 1273, Seton, East Lothian, marries 1) Gartnait or Grateny, Earl of Mar, 1292 in Kildrummy, Aberdeenshire, 2) Sir Christopher Seton, put to death by English in 1306, 3) Sir Andrew Moray, 20 Septemb
2 CONC er 1305, d. 1356/7 in Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Robert''' b 11 July 1274, married 1) Isabella of Mar, 2) Elizabeth de Burgh, d June 7, 1329 
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Neil''' (Niall or Nigel), taken prisoner at Kildrummie, hanged and beheaded at Berwick-upon-Tweed in September 1306.
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Edward''', crowned May 2, 1316, 'King of Ireland'. Killed in battle, October 5, 1318. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Mary''', married (1) Sir. Neil Campbell; (2) Sir. Alexander Fraser of Touchfraser and Cowie. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Margaret''' m. Sir William Carlyle
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Thomas''', taken prisoner in Galloway, executed 9 February 1307, Carlisle, Cumberland[32] 
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Alexander''', executed 9 February 1307, Carlisle, Cumberland 
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Elizabeth''' m. Sir William Dishington of Ardross
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Matilda''' married Hugh / Aodh, Earl of Ross, in 1308 Orkney Isles, dies after September 1323 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ex-Spouse: Maud FitzAlan
2 CONT
2 CONT See this [https://www.geni.com/discussions/180288?msg=1211106 disussion] for a copy of Douglas Richardson's 2018 post in soc.genealogy.medieval which provides evidence for this marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son and heir of Robert de Brus, 5th Lord of Annandale and Isobel de Clare, daughter of the Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, his birth date is generally accepted (July 1243) but there is a ongoing debate as to whether Robert an
2 CONC d / or his son King Robert The Bruce, were born on the family estate at Writtle Essex.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend tells that the 27-year-old Robert de Brus, was a handsome young man participating in the Ninth Crusade. when Adam de Kilconquhar, one of his companion-in-arms, fell in 1270, at Acre, and Robert was obliged to travel to tell
2 CONC  the sad news to Adam's widow Marjorie of Carrick. The story continues that Marjorie was so taken with the messenger that she had him held captive until he agreed to marry her, which he did in 1271. However, since the crusade land
2 CONC ed in Acre on May 9th 1271, and only started to engage the Muslims in late June, the story and his participation in the Ninth Crusade are generally discounted.
2 CONT
2 CONT What is recorded, is that in:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1264 He had to ransom his own father, after his capture, along with Henry III, Richard of Cornwall, and Edward I at the Battle of Lewes, Sussex. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1271 He married, without Scottish Royal consent, Marjory, countess of Carrick. As a result she temporarily loses her castle and estates, regained on payment of a fine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1274 Jul-Sep He is present, along with Alexander III, his Queen Margaret, their children and 100 Scottish lords and knights at the Coronation and accompanying celebrations of Edward I, at Westminster. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1278 He swears fealty to Edward I, on behalf of Alexander III at Westminster. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Accompanies Alexander III to Tewkesbury 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1281 He is part of the delegation to Guy of Flanders, to arrange the marriage of the Lord Alexander. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1282 He participates and is paid for his services in Edward's Conquest of Wales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1283 June, he is summoned by writ to Shrewsbury, for the trial of David ap Griffith. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1285 Jun 1 - Earl & Countess, at Turnberry, grant the men of Melrose abbey certain freedoms, according to English law. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1286 He is witness, along with his son Robert, to the grant of the church of Campbeltown to Paisley Abbey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1290 He is party to the Treaty of Birgham. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He supports his father's claim to the vacant throne of Scotland, left so on the death of Margaret I of Scotland in 1290. The initial civil proceedings, known as The Great Cause, awarded the Crown to his 2nd cousin and rival, John 
2 CONC Balliol. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1291 He swears fealty to Edward I as overlord of Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1292 His wife Marjorie dies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT November, his father, Robert de Brus, 5th Lord of Annandale - the unsuccessful claimant - resigns his Lordship of Annandale, and claim to the throne to him, allegedly to avoid having to swear fealty to John. In turn he passes his 
2 CONC own Earldom of Carrick, in fee, on to his son Robert. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1293 January 1st - His warrener at Gt. Badow, a Richard, is caught poaching venison at Northle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1293 He sets sail for Norway, for the marriage of his daughter Isabel to King Eric II of Norway. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1294/5 He returns to England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1295 His father dies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Oct 6, swears fealty to Edward and is made Constable of Carlisle Castle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Refuses a summons to the Scottish host. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Annandale is seized, by King John Balliol, and given to John 'The Red' Comyn, Lord of Badenoch. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Confirms, to Guisborough Priory, the churches of Annandale and Hart. Witnessed by Walter de Fauconberg and Marmaduke de Thweng. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Exchanges common pasture, for land held by William of Carlisle at Kinmount. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Exchanges land in Estfield, for a field adjacent to the prior of Hatfield Regis's manor at Brunesho End Broomshawbury. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grants Robert Taper, and his wife Millicent, a messuage in Hatfield Regis, and via a separate grant 5.5 acres of arable and 1 acre of meadow, in Hatfield Regis, for 16s annual rent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grants John de Bledelowe, the former lands / tenement of Richard de Cumbes, in Hatfield Regis, for 1d annual rent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alters the terms of a grant to Richard de Fanwreyte, of Folewelleshaleyerde, Montpeliers, Writtle, from services to an annual rent. Witnesses includes two of Roberts Cook's at Writtle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alters the terms of a grant to Stephen the Tanner, of Folewelleshaleyerde, Montpeliers, Writtle, from services to an annual rent. Witnesses includes two of Roberts Cook's at Writtle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alters the terms of a grant to Willam Mayhew, of the tenement Barrieland, Hatfield Regis, to an annual rent of 5s and some services. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1296 Jan, He is summoned to attend to the King Edward at Salisbury 
2 CONT
2 CONT April 28th, he again swears fealty to Edward I and fights for Edward, at the Battle of Dunbar Castle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT August, with his son Robert he renews the pledge of homage and fealty to Edward, at the 'victory parliament’ in Berwick. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I denies his claim to the throne and he retires to his estates in Essex. 
2 CONT
2 CONT August 29 - At Berwick, agrees the dower lands of his widowed step mother, Christina. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Annandale is re-gained. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1298 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan 7 - Transfers a grant of land at Hatfield Regis, from Walter Arnby to his son William.. 
2 CONT
2 CONT May 29 - Grants a John Herolff a half virgate of land in Writtle[20]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1299 
2 CONT
2 CONT Feburary 1 - Rents lands at Hatfield Regis, Essex to a John de Bledelowe, for 4s anual rent . 
2 CONT
2 CONT August 4 - While resident at Writtle, he Rents lands at Hatfield Regis, Essex to a Nicholas de Barenton, for 21s anual rent . 
2 CONT
2 CONT c. 1300
2 CONT
2 CONT Marries an Eleanor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1301 November 26 - Grants, Bunnys in Hatfield Broad Oak and Takeley, to a Edward Thurkyld. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1301, Enfeoffments Writtle to a John de Lovetot and his wife Joan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1304 Easter, dies on route to Annandale and is buried at Holm Cultram Abbey, Cumberland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his death his Eleanor remarries, before February 8, 1306 (as his 1st wife) Richard Waleys, Lord Waleys, and they had issue. She died shortly before September 8, 1331. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the Battle of Stirling Bridge (1297), Annandale was wasted as retaliation to younger Bruce's actions. Yet, when Edward returned to England after his victory at the Battle of Falkirk, which one source accords to Rober
2 CONC t turning the Scottish flank, Annandale and Carrick were excepted from the lordships and lands which he assigned to his followers, father having not opposed Edward and the son being treated as a waverer whose allegiance might stil
2 CONC l be retained.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert at that time was old and ill, and there are reports that he wished his son to seek peace with Edward. If not his son's actions could jeopardise his own income, which was primarily derived from his holding's south of the bor
2 CONC der (est. £340 vs £150[28]). The elder Bruce would have seen that, if the rebellion failed and his son were against Edward, the son would lose everything, titles, lands, and probably his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was around this time (1302) that Robert's son submits to Edward I. The younger Robert had sided with the Scottish nationalists since the capture and exile of Balliol. There are many reasons which may have prompted his return to
2 CONC  Edward, not the least of which was that the Bruce family may have found it loathsome to continue sacrificing his followers, family and inheritance for King John. There were rumours that John would return with a French army and re
2 CONC gain the Scottish throne. Soulis supported his return as did many other nobles, but this would lead to the Bruces losing any chance of ever gaining the throne themselves.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife was by all accounts a formidable woman. Marjorie was the daughter and heiress of Niall, 2nd Earl of Carrick. Carrick was a Gaelic Earldom in Southern Scotland. Its territories contained much of today's Ayrshire and 
2 CONC Dumfriesshire. The couple held the principal seats of Turnberry Castle and Lochmaben.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bruce in fiction
2 CONT
2 CONT He was portrayed (as a leper) by Ian Bannen in the 1995 film Braveheart. Braveheart wrongly portrays Robert de Brus as being involved in the capture of William Wallace in Edinburgh; as noted above this Robert de Brus died in 1304 
2 CONC and William Wallace was captured on August 3, 1305 by Sir John de Menteith in Glasgow.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Notes
