2 CONT
2 CONT Ollum monnum þeim som þetta bref sia æder høræ sendæ Þorer pre(s)ter a Saudum Solui Ormarson ok Rorlæiuer Biornson :q: g: ok sinæ kunnikt gerande at a tolftæ are vars verdulix herra b.XI s.91 Erix med [gudz1 nad Norex konongz hørd
2 CONC om ok *sadom at Soluir Ormarson seldi Þoreuildi Þorgeirs/son/ v mar(ka) bool .j. Æpplæzwetum frelst ok hemult firir *þiariom manne med lutum ok lunnidum som tilliger ok leget hauer fra forno ok nygiu vten garzs ok jnnan ok som lig
2 CONC ger .j.j. Brvggæbergæsokn ok þo .j.j. Lofgardenom .j.j. fyr nemdæ sokn kiendiz þa firirnemder Solui at han hafde vpboret firstæ penig(e)n ok æf(s)tæ som .j.j. kaup þeiræ kom. til [sanende1 setom mit okor jnsigli meder Solues firne
2 CONC mdom firir þetta bref er gort var degi ok are som fir sægir
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Tilskrevet over Linien. tilbake
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.dokpro.uio.no/perl/middelalder/diplom_vise_tekst.prl?b=6088&s=n&str=
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He belonged to the lower nobility. 1 June 1442 he was one of 36 delegates from “the ordinary people” to a national meeting confirming the election of Kristoffer of Bavaria as the new King of Norway,  Sweden and Denmark. His name 
2 CONC Solve means “solved” or “saved”. The tradition is that it was given  to him because he survived the great plague The Black Death of 1349/50; just after he was born.  Another version is that it was his mother who survived the plagu
2 CONC e, and that he was born prematurely just afterwards, but nevertheless grew up. Since he was an active man as late as in 1442, I find it  less likely that he was born as early as ca. 1350. Hence, I must assume that if the traditio
2 CONC n has a core  of truth, it must be about one of the plagues in the decades after 1350. His ancestry here is based  on the tradition, that is supported by known relations in the following generations.
