0 @N6000000003051240865@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "James I of Aragon", retrieved 16 Oct 07]
1 CONT James I the Conqueror (Catalan: Jaume el Conqueridor, Aragonese: Chaime lo Conqueridor, Spanish: Jaime el Conquistador, Occitan: Jacme lo Conquistaire; 2 February 1208 - 27 July 1276) was the King of Aragon, Count of Barcelona, an
1 CONC d Lord of Montpellier from 1213 to 1276. His long reign saw the expansion of the Crown of Aragon on all sides: into Valencia to the south, Languedoc to the north, and the Balearic Islands to the east. By a treaty with Louis IX of 
1 CONC France, he wrested the Principality of Catalonia from nominal French suzerainty and integrated it into his crown. His part in the Reconquista was similar in Mediterranean Spain to that of his contemporary Ferdinand III of Castile 
1 CONC in Andalusia.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a legislator and organiser, he occupies a high place among the Spanish kings. James compiled the Libre del Consulat de Mar, which governed maritime trade and helped establish Catalan supremacy in the western Mediterranean. He m
1 CONC ade Catalan the official language of his domains and sponsored Catalan literature, even a quasi-autobiographical chronicle of his reign: the Llibre dels fets.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early life and reign until majority
1 CONT James was born at Montpellier as the only child of Peter II and Mary, heiress of William VIII of Montpellier and Eudokia Komnene. As a child, James was a pawn in the power politics of Provence, where his father was engaged in stru
1 CONC ggles with the Cathar heretics of Albi on one side and the Albigensian Crusaders led by Simon IV de Montfort, Earl of Leicester, who were trying to exterminate them on the other. Peter endeavoured to placate the northern crusaders
1 CONC  by arranging a marriage between his son James and Simon's daughter. He entrusted the boy to be educated in Montfort's care in 1211, but was soon forced to take up arms against him, dying at the Battle of Muret on 12 September 121
1 CONC 3. Montfort would willingly have used James as a means of extending his own power had not the Aragonese and Catalans appealed to Pope Innocent III, who insisted that Montfort surrender him. James was handed over, at Carcassonne, i
1 CONC n May or June 1214, to the papal legate Peter of Benevento.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was then sent to Monzón, where he was entrusted to the care of William of Montredon, the head of the Knights Templar in Spain and Provence; the regency meanwhile fell to his great uncle Sancho, Count of Roussillon, and his s
1 CONC on, the king's cousin, Nuño. The kingdom was given over to confusion until, in 1217, the Templars and some of the more loyal nobles brought the young king to Zaragoza.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1221, he was married to Eleanor, daughter of Alfonso VIII of Castile and Leonora of England. The next six years of his reign were full of rebellions on the part of the nobles. By the Peace of Alcalá of 31 March 1227, the nobles
1 CONC  and the king came to terms.
1 CONT
1 CONT Acquisition of Urgell
1 CONT In 1228, James faced the sternest opposition from a vassal yet. Guerau de Cabrera had occupied the County of Urgell in opposition to Aurembiax, the heiress of Ermengol VIII, who had died without sons in 1208. While Aurembiax' moth
1 CONC er, Elvira, had made herself a protegée of James' father, on her death (1220), Guerao had occupied the county and displaced Aurembiax, claiming that a woman could not inherit.
1 CONT
1 CONT James intervened on behalf of Aurembiax, whom he owed protection. He bought Guerau off and allowed Aurembiax to reclaim her territory, which she did at Lleida, probably also becoming one of James' earliest mistresses. She surrende
1 CONC red Lleida to James and agreed to hold Urgell in fief from him. On her death in 1231, James exchanged the Balearic Islands for Urgell with her widower, Peter of Portugal.
1 CONT
1 CONT Relations with France and Navarre
1 CONT From 1230 to 1232, James negotiated with Sancho VII of Navarre, who desired his help against his nephew and closest living male relative, Theobald IV of Champagne. James and Sancho negotiated a treaty whereby James would inherit N
1 CONC avarre on the old Sancho's death, but when this did occur, the Navarrese nobless instead elevated Theobald to the throne (1234), and James disputed it. Pope Gregory IX was required to intervene. In the end, James accepted Theobald
1 CONC 's succession.
1 CONT
1 CONT James endeavoured to form a state straddling the Pyrenees, to counterbalance the power of France north of the Loire. As with the much earlier Visigothic attempt, this policy was victim to physical, cultural, and political obstacle
1 CONC s. As in the case of Navarre, he was too wise to launch into perilous adventures. By the Treaty of Corbeil, signed in May 1258, he frankly withdrew from conflict with Louis IX of France and was content with the recognition of his 
1 CONC position, and the surrender of antiquated and illusory French claims to the overlordship of Catalonia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reconquista
1 CONT After his false start at uniting Aragon with the Kingdom of Navarre through a scheme of mutual adoption, James turned to the south and the Mediterranean Sea, where he conquered the Balearic Islands (Majorca 1229; Minorca 1232; Ibi
1 CONC za 1235) and where Valencia capitulated 28 September 1238.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his remaining two decades after Corbeil, James warred with the Moors in Murcia, on behalf of his son-in-law Alfonso X of Castile. On 26 March 1244, the two monarchs signed the Treaty of Almizra to determine the zones of the
1 CONC ir expansion into Andalusia so as to prevent squabbling between them. Specifically, it defined the borders of the newly-created Kingdom of Valencia. James signed it on that date, but Alfonso did not affirm it until much later. Acc
1 CONC ording to the treaty, all lands south of a line from Biar to Villajoyosa through Busot were reserved for Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Patronage of art, learning, and literature
1 CONT James built and consecrated the Cathedral of Lleida, which was constructed in a style transitional between Romanesque and Gothic with little influence from Moorish styles.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was a patron of the University of Montpellier, which owed much of its development to his impetus. He also founded a studium at Valencia in 1245 and received privileges for it from Pope Innocent IV, but it did not develop as 
1 CONC splendidly. In 1263, James presided over a debate in Barcelona between the Jewish rabbi Nahmanides and Pablo Christiani, a prominent converso.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was the first great sponsor and patron of vernacular Catalan literature. Indeed, he may himself be called "the first of the Catalan prose writers." James wrote or dictated at various stages a chronicle of his own life, Llibr
1 CONC e dels fets in Catalan, which is the first self-chronicle of a Christian king. As well as a fine example of autobiography the "Book of Deeds" expresses concepts of the power and purpose of monarchy; examples of loyalty and treache
1 CONC ry in the feudal order; the growth of national sentiment based on homeland, language, and culture; and medieval military tactics.
1 CONT
1 CONT James also wrote the Libre de la Saviesa or "Book of Wisdom." The book contains proverbs from various authors going back as far as King Solomon and as close to his own time, such as Albert the Great. It even contains maxims from t
1 CONC he medieval Arab philosophers and from the Apophthegmata Philosophorum of Honein ben Ishak, which was probably translated at Barcelona during his reign. A Hebrew translator by the name of Jehuda was employed at James's court durin
1 CONC g this period.
1 CONT
1 CONT Though James was himself a prose writer and sponsored mostly prose works, he had an appreciation of verse. In consequence of the Albigensian Crusade, many troubadours were forced to flee southern France and many found refuge in Ar
1 CONC agon and Catalonia. Notwithstanding his early patronage of poetry, by the influence of his confessor Ramon de Penyafort, James brought the Inquisition into his realm in 1233 to prevent any vernacular translation of the Bible.
1 CONT
1 CONT Succession
1 CONT The favour James showed his illegitimate offspring led to protest from the nobles, and to conflicts between his sons legitimate and illegitimate. When one of the latter, Fernán Sánchez, who had behaved with gross ingratitude and t
1 CONC reason to his father, was slain by the legitimate son Peter, the old king recorded his grim satisfaction.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the close of his life, James divided his states between his sons by Yolanda of Hungary: the aforementioned Peter received the Hispanic possessions on the mainland and James, the Kingdom of Majorca (including the Balearic Island
1 CONC s and the counties of Roussillon and Cerdanya) and the Lordship of Montpellier. The division inevitably produced fratricidal conflicts. In 1276, the king fell very ill at Alzira and resigned his crown, intending to retire to the m
1 CONC onastery of Poblet, but he died at Valencia on 27 July.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and children
1 CONT James first married, in 1221, Eleanor, daughter of Alfonso VIII of Castile and Leonora of England. Though he later had the marriage annulled, his one son by her was declared legitimate:
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso (1229-1260), married Constance of Montcada, Countess of Bigorre
1 CONT In 1235, James remarried to Yolanda, daughter of Andrew II of Hungary by his second wife Yolande de Courtenay. She bore him numerous children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Yolanda, also known as Violant, (1236-1301), married Alfonso X of Castile
1 CONT Constance (1239-1269), married Juan Manuel, Lord of Villena, son of Ferdinand III
1 CONT Peter (1240-1285), successor in Aragon, Catalonia, and Valencia
1 CONT James (1243-1311), successor in Balearics and Languedoc
1 CONT Ferdinand (1245-1250)
1 CONT Sancha (1246-1251)
1 CONT Isabella (1247-1271), married Philip III of France
1 CONT Mary (1248-1267), nun
1 CONT Sancho, Archbishop of Toledo (1250-1279)
1 CONT Eleanor (born 1251, died young)
1 CONT James married thirdly Teresa Gil de Vidaure, but only by a private document, and left her when she developed leprosy.
1 CONT
1 CONT James (c.1255?1285), lord of Xèrica
1 CONT Peter (1259?1318), lord of Ayerbe
1 CONT James also had several lovers, both during and after his marriages, and a few bore him illegitimate sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT By Blanca d'Antillón:
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferran Sanchis (or Fernando Sánchez; 1240?1275), baron of Castro
1 CONT By Berenguela Fernández:
1 CONT
1 CONT Pedro Fernández, baron of Híjar
1 CONT By Elvira Sarroca:
1 CONT
1 CONT Jaume Sarroca (born 1248), Archbishop of Huesca
0 @N6000000003637326197@ NOTE Jaime I de Aragón (Montpellier, 2 de febrero de 1208 - Alcira, 27 de julio de 1276) fue rey de Aragón (1213 - 1276), de Valencia (1239-76) y de Mallorca (1229-1276), conde de Barcelona (1213-1276), señor de Montpellier (1219-1276)
1 CONC  y de otros feudos en Occitania.
