0 @N6000000002187742701@ NOTE Henry I Sinclair, Earl of Orkney
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I Sinclair, Earl of Orkney, Baron of Roslin, and Lord of Shetland (c.1345-c.1400), was a Scottish explorer and nobleman. He is sometimes identified by the alternative spelling Henry St Clair. He was the grandfather of Willia
1 CONC m Sinclair, the builder of Rosslyn Chapel. He is also noted for being the subject of legend that he undertook early explorations of Greenland and North America in about the year 1398. According to a biography published many years 
1 CONC after his death, he died in battle against the English around the year 1400.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Life
1 CONT     * 2 The Sinclair voyage to America?
1 CONT     * 3 Sinclair's Voyage and the Knights Templar
1 CONT     * 4 Criticisms of this theory
1 CONT           o 4.1 Sinclairs and the Templars
1 CONT     * 5 References
1 CONT           o 5.1 Books
1 CONT           o 5.2 Web
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was born as son and heir of William Sinclair, Lord of Rosslyn, and Isobel of Strathearn, a daughter of Maol Iosa, Earl of Orkney (Malise of Strathearn) and his designated heiress of the Orcadian principality. Henry's materna
1 CONC l grandfather had been deposed of much of his lands (earldom of Strathearn was completely lost to scottish king), and attempts of descendants to recover those played a remarkable role in Henry's life.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his fathers death in 1358, Henry succeeded as laird of Rosslyn. He pursued to reclaiming at least some of the Norse lands that had been divided between heirs of earl Malise. Orkney however was held by an aunt's husband, the S
1 CONC wede Erengisle Suneson, and after him rivalling relatives.
1 CONT
1 CONT Three cousins: Alexander de L'Arde, Lord of Caithness; Malise Sparre, Lord of Skaldale; and Henry, rivalled in succession. On 2 August 1379 at Marstrand, near Tonsberg, Norway, king Haakon VI of Norway, Orkney's technical liege lo
1 CONC rd, invested and confirmed Henry as the Norwegian earl of Orkney over the rival claim by his cousin Malise Sparre.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1389, earl Henry attended the coronation of king Eric the Pomeranian in Norway, and pledged his oath of fealty. In 1391, he occupied the Faroese Islands to his principality.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1391, earl Henry and his troops slayed Malise Sparre near Scalloway, Tingwall, Shetland, and he secured his dominion of Shetland islands.
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl Henry was in reality in rather independent position, being able to act like a little king in his archipelago realm of North Atlantic.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Sinclair voyage to America?
1 CONT
1 CONT Little else is known about Sinclair's life. Much has been written through conjecture, however, about his possible career as an explorer. In particular, starting in the 19th century, he was identified by Johann Reinhold Forster as 
1 CONC possibly being the prince Zichmni described in letters allegedly written around the year 1400 by the Zeno brothers of Venice, in which they describe a voyage throughout the North Atlantic under the command of Zichmni. [1]
1 CONT
1 CONT The authenticity of the letters (which were not discovered and published until the 16th century), the exact course of the voyage, as well as whether or not it even occurred, has not been firmly established. Many historians regard 
1 CONC the letters (and the accompanying map) as a hoax, either by the Zeno brothers or by the descendant who later published them. Moreover, the identification of Zichmni as Henry Sinclair is very controversial, although it is often tak
1 CONC en for granted among supporters of the theory.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most controversial theories speculate that Henry (Zichmni) traveled not only to Greenland but to present-day Nova Scotia, where he may have founded a settlement among the Micmac Indians, and perhaps as far south as present-day
1 CONC  Massachusetts and Rhode Island. According to these theories, his expedition may have been responsible for the building of the Newport Tower and the carving of the Westford Knight.
1 CONT
1 CONT The theory that Henry Sinclair explored North America is based on three separate propositions:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. That the letters and map ascribed to the Zeno brothers and published in 1558 are authentic.
1 CONT    2. That the voyage described in the letters taken by Zichmni around the year 1398 actually reached North America.
1 CONT    3. That the Zichmni is Henry Sinclair.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sinclair's Voyage and the Knights Templar
1 CONT
1 CONT Intertwined with the Sinclair voyage story is the theory that Henry Sinclair was a Knight Templar and that the voyage either was sponsored by or conducted on the behalf of the Templars.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Templar connection hinges on the fact that the name Nova Scotia is Latin for New Scotland. This is considered significant because adherents of the theory believe that some Templars escaped the suppression of their order by Phi
1 CONC lip the Fair (Philippe le Bel, 1307) by fleeing to Scotland[3]. The Knights Templar were publicly disbanded in the early 1300s, after the arrest in France of thousands of their members by the King of France[4].
1 CONT
1 CONT The Theory, sometimes known as "The La Merika Theory" [5] also hinges on the contention that there are stone carvings of American plants in Rosslyn Chapel in Scotland,[6] plants supposedly not seen by Europeans until Columbus. The
1 CONC  Chapel was build by Henry Sinclair's grandson William Sinclair and was completed in 1486. Columbus made his first voyage in 1492. This is seen by authors Christopher Knight and Robert Lomas as being compelling evidence for the th
1 CONC eory that Henry sailed to America.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Criticisms of this theory
1 CONT
1 CONT One primary criticism of this theory is that if either a Sinclair or a Templar voyage reached the Americas, they did not, unlike Columbus, return with a historical record of their findings. Apart from the graveyards in Nova Scotia
1 CONC  and the carvings in the Rosslyn Chapel, there is no physical evidence that could prove that Templars or Sinclair had explored the Americas. Advocates of the theory contend that it is possible that this lack of documentation can b
1 CONC e explained by a strong motivation of the Sinclair voyagers to keep their activities secret[8]. Historians question this. They also question the authenticity of the Westford Knight, claiming that it is not clearly inscribed, and m
1 CONC ay be a hoax or a result of erosion that makes it appear to resemble a knight. Finally, the carvings in Rosslyn Chapel may not be of American plants at all, and according to one historian are nothing more than stylized carvings of
1 CONC  wheat and strawberries.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sinclairs and the Templars
1 CONT
1 CONT Historians Mark Oxbrow, Ian Robertson,[10] Karen Ralls and Louise Yeoman [11] have each made it clear that the Sinclair family had no connection with the Mediaeval Knights Templar. The Sinclairs' testimony against the Knights at t
1 CONC heir 1309 trial is not consistent with any alleged support or membership. In "The Templars and the Grail"[12] Karen Ralls states that among some 50 who testified against the Templars were Henry and William Sinclair.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ [1]
1 CONT    2. ^ "The Lost Treasure of the Knights Templar: New Light on the Oak Island Mystery" by Steven Sora, Atlantis Rising Magazine #20, 1999
1 CONT    3. ^ The Second Messiah: Templars, the Turin Shroud and the Great Secret of Freemasonry, by Christopher Knight and Robert Lomas, Fair Winds Press, August 1, 2001, ISBN 1-931412-76-6
1 CONT    4. ^ The Second Messiah: Templars, the Turin Shroud and the Great Secret of Freemasonry, by Christopher Knight and Robert Lomas, Fair Winds Press, August 1, 2001, ISBN 1-931412-76-6'
1 CONT    5. ^ "A Scottish knight templar, Henry Sinclair, seeking refuge from the suppression of his order in 1398. He and his freemasons escaped with assorted treasures and holy grails to settle in Nova Scotia with the Micmac Indians (
1 CONC clearly a tribe of Hiberno-Scots ancestry). Sinclair's masonic star, or "la merika", duly gave its name to the continent and merits a Da Vinci saga all of its own. The only blot on this glory is that everyone knows America got its
1 CONC  name from Glamorgan's Richard ap Meurig (Amerik), a wealthy sponsor of John Cabot's search for the north-west passage in the 1490s." Simon Jenkins,The Guardian, Friday January 20, 2006
1 CONT    6. ^ The Hiram Key, Fair winds Press, ISBN 1-59233-159-9, Knight and Lomas
1 CONT    7. ^ Knight and Lomas The Hyram Key
1 CONT    8. ^ In The Hiram Key many references are made to entrusted secrets, which the authors claims were encoded in the architecture of the Sinclair's Rosslyn Chapel
1 CONT
1 CONT           Insert the text of the quote here, without quotation marks.
1 CONT
1 CONT    9. ^ Historian Mark Oxbrow, quoted in "The ship of dreams" by Diane MaClean, Scotsman.com, 13 May, 2005
1 CONT   10. ^ "The Da Vinci Connection", Sunday Herald, 14 November 2004
1 CONT   11. ^ "Historian attacks Rosslyn Chapel for 'cashing in on Da Vinci Code'", Scotsman.com, 03-May-06
1 CONT   12. ^ Ralls, Karen - "The Templars and the Grail" - Quest Books; 1st Quest edition (May 25, 2003), ISBN 0-8356-0807-7 (see p.110 - quoting "The Knights Templar in England" p.200-1)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Books
1 CONT
1 CONT     * "From Jamestown to Texas: A History of Some Early Pioneers of Austin County the Colonial Capitol...", by Betty Smith Meischen, iUniverse, 2002, ISBN 0-595-24223-5 Google Books link to page 498
1 CONT     * "Second Messiah: Templars, the Turin Shroud and the Great Secret of Freemasonry", by Christopher Knight and Robert Lomas, Fair Winds Press, August 1, 2001, ISBN 1-931412-76-6
1 CONT     * "Prince Henry Sinclair: His Expedition to the New World in 1398", 1974 and 1995, by Frederick J. Pohl, Clarkson N. Potter, New York, ISBN 1-55109-122-4.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Web
1 CONT
1 CONT     * "Of course the Chinese didn't discover America. But then nor did Columbus" by Simon Jenkins, January 20, 2006 article in The Guardian mentioning the La Merika theory among others
1 CONT     * "The ship of dreams" by Diane MacLean, May 13, 2005, Scotsman.com
1 CONT     * Prince Henry Sinclair Society of North America This society celebrates the visit of Prince Henry Sinclair from Orkney to America in 1398.
1 CONT     * "The Sinclair Voyage to America" Renaissance Magazine #12, 1999
1 CONT     * "The Lost Treasure of the Knights Templar: New Light on the Oak Island Mystery" by Steven Sora, Atlantis Rising Magazine #20, 1999
1 CONT     * Brief biography in support of theory
1 CONT     * Report of 1997 symposium declaring the 1398 expedition Not Proven
1 CONT     * Refutation of Henry-as-Zichmni theory
1 CONT
