0 @N6000000002188179374@ NOTE Alfonso X of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso X (November 23, 1221, Toledo, Spain – April 4, 1284, Seville, Spain) was a Spanish monarch who ruled as the King of Galicia, Castile and León from 1252 until his death. He was elected Rex Romanorum in 1254. His nicknames w
1 CONC ere "el Sabio" ("the Wise", more accurately translated "the Learned") and "el Astrólogo" ("the Astronomer").
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Life
1 CONT     * 2 Family
1 CONT     * 3 Additional notes
1 CONT     * 4 References and Notes
1 CONT     * 5 Further Reading
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Statue of Alfonso X in Madrid (J. Alcoverro, 1892).
1 CONT Statue of Alfonso X in Madrid (J. Alcoverro, 1892).
1 CONT Monument to Alfonso X in La Puebla del Río, Seville
1 CONT Monument to Alfonso X in La Puebla del Río, Seville
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Alfonso was the eldest son of Ferdinand III of Castile and Beatriz of Swabia. His maternal grandparents were Philip of Swabia and Irene Angelina.
1 CONT     * As a writer and intellectual he gained considerable scientific fame based on his encouragement of astronomy and the Ptolemaic cosmology as known to him through the Arabs. (Because of this, the Alphonsus crater on the Moon is
1 CONC  named after him). His fame extends to the preparation of the Alfonsine tables,based on calculations of al-Zarqali Alzarquel. One famous quote attributed to him was supposedly said upon hearing an explanation of Ptolemy's theory o
1 CONC f astronomy and being shown the extremely complicated mathematics required to "prove" it - "If the Lord Almighty had consulted me before embarking on creation thus, I should have recommended something simpler." The validity of thi
1 CONC s quotation is questioned by some historians.[1]
1 CONT     * Alfonso established in Seville, Spain a translation school that did a great work increasing the flow of knowledge into Christian Europe as well as continuing support of the school of translators in Toledo (already founded 11
1 CONC 27-1152 by Archbishop Raimondo of Toledo). Much of it was based on Ancient philosophy.
1 CONT     * As a ruler, Alfonso showed legislative capacity, and a wish to provide his kingdoms with a code of laws and a consistent judicial system. The Fuero Real was undoubtedly his work. He began the code called the Siete Partidas, 
1 CONC which, however, was only promulgated by his great-grandson. Because of this, he is one of the 23 lawmakers depicted in the chamber of the United States House of Representatives.
1 CONT     * Alfonso was the first king who initiated the use of the Castilian language extensively, although his father, Fernando III had begun to use it for some documents, instead of Latin, as the language used in courts, churches, an
1 CONC d in books and official documents.
1 CONT     * Alfonso lacked the singleness of purpose required by a ruler who would devote himself to organization, and also the combination of firmness with temper needed for dealing with his nobles.
1 CONT     * Alfonso's descent from the Hohenstaufen through his mother, a daughter of the emperor Philip of Swabia, gave him a claim to represent the Swabian line. Alfonso's election by the prince-electors of the Holy Roman Empire after
1 CONC  the death of Conrad IV of Germany in 1254 misled him into wild schemes that involved excessive expense but never took effect. To obtain money, he debased the coinage and then endeavoured to prevent a rise in prices by an arbitrar
1 CONC y tariff. The little trade of his dominions was ruined, and the burghers and peasants were deeply offended. His nobles, whom he tried to cow by sporadic acts of violence, rebelled against him.
1 CONT     * Alfonso's eldest son, Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile, died in 1275, leaving two infant sons. Alfonso's second son, Sancho, claimed to be the new heir, in preference to the children of Ferdinand de la Cerda, basing
1 CONC  his claim on an old Castilian custom, that of proximity of blood and agnatic seniority. Alfonso preferred to leave the throne to his grandsons, but Sancho had the support of the nobility. A bitter civil war broke out resulting in
1 CONC  1282 Alfonso's being forced to accept Sancho as his heir instead of his young grandsons.Son and nobles alike supported the Moors when he tried to unite the nation in a crusade; and when he allied himself with Abu Yusuf Yakub, the
1 CONC  ruling Marinid Sultan of Morocco, they denounced him as an enemy of the faith. A reaction in his favor was beginning in his later days, but he died defeated and deserted at Seville, leaving a will, by which he endeavored to exclu
1 CONC de Sancho, and a heritage of civil war.
1 CONT     * Alfonso X commissioned or co-authored numerous works during his reign. These works included Cantigas d'escarnio e maldicer, General Estoria and the Libro de los juegos ("Book of Games").
1 CONT     * Among the most important of the works by Alfonso X was the celebrated Cantigas de Santa Maria ("Songs to the Virgin Mary"), one of the largest collections of monophonic songs to survive from Medieval times. The Cantigas de S
1 CONC anta Maria consists of 420 poems written in Galician-Portuguese with musical notation. The poems are for the most part on miracles attributed to the Virgin Mary One of the miracles Alfonso relates is his own healing in Puerto de S
1 CONC anta María.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1246, Alfonso X married Violante of Aragon, the daughter of King James I of Aragon and Yolande of Hungary in 1249, although betrothed already in 1246. Because of her young age (Violante was only 13-years-old at the time of the 
1 CONC marriage), she produced no children for several years and it was feared that she was barren. Alfonso almost had their marriage annulled, but they went on to have twelve children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Fernando, died in infancy, and buried in Las Huelgas in Burgos.
1 CONT    2. Berengaria of Castile (1253-after 1284). She was betrothed to Louis, the son and heir of King Louis IX of France, but her fiance died prematurely in 1260. She entered the convent in Las Huelgas, where she was living in 1284.
1 CONT    3. Beatriz of Castile (1254-1280). She married William VII, Marquess of Montferrat.
1 CONT    4. Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile (October 23, 1255-July 25, 1275). He married Blanche, the daughter of King Louis IX of France, by whom he had two children. Because he predeceased his father, his younger brother San
1 CONC cho inherited the throne.
1 CONT    5. Leonor of Castile (1257-1275)
1 CONT    6. Sancho IV of Castile (May 13, 1258-1295)
1 CONT    7. Constanza of Castile (1258-August 22, 1280), a nun at Las Huelgas.
1 CONT    8. Pedro of Castile (June 1260-October 10, 1283)
1 CONT    9. Juan of Castile, Lord of Valencia (March or April, 1262-June 25, 1319).
1 CONT   10. Isabella, died young.
1 CONT   11. Violante of Castile (1265-1296). She married Diego Lopez de Haro
1 CONT   12. Jaime of Castile (August 1266-August 9, 1284)
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso X also had several illegitimate children. His illegitimate daughter, Beatriz de Castilla, married King Alfonso III of Portugal. An illegitimate son, Martin, was Abbot of Valladolid.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Additional notes
1 CONT
1 CONT A portrait of Alfonso X hangs in the chamber of the United States House of Representatives.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Order of Alfonso X of Castile
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References and Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Owen Gingerich, "Alfonso X as Patron of Astronomy."
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ballesteros-Beretta, Antonio. Alfonso X el Sabio, 1963
1 CONT     * Gingerich, Owen. "Alfonso X as a Patron of Astronomy." The Eye of Heaven: Ptolemy, Copernicus, Kepler. New York: American Institute of Physics, 1993.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Further Reading
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Thomas, Phillip Drennon. (1970). "Alfonso el Sabio". Dictionary of Scientific Biography 1: 122. New York: Charles Scribner's Sons.
1 CONT
