1 CONT Giske. Skjalg het en rik og mektig mann, som også kom med i flokken. ...?
0 @N6000000002187829167@ NOTE Albert the Fat, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert (Latin Albertus; died 22 September 1318), called the Fat (pinguis), was duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT The second son of Albert the Tall, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Albert was a boy when his father died in 1279. He was first under guardianship of his uncle, Conrad, Bishop of Verden, and then of his elder brother, Henry the Strange
1 CONC , Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg. In 1286 the three brothers divided their father's territory; Albert received the areas around Göttingen, Minden, Northeim, Calenberg, and Hanover. He made Göttingen his residence. In 1292, the third b
1 CONC rother, William, died childless, and Albert and Henry quarrelled about William's share, the areas around Brunswick and Wolfenbüttel; Albert finally prevailed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert married Rixa, daughter of Henry I, Prince of Werle, in 1284. They had the following children that reached adulthood:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Adelaide, married John, Landgrave of Lower Hesse
1 CONT     * Richenza, Abbess of Gandersheim
1 CONT     * Mechtild
1 CONT     * Jutta
1 CONT     * Luder, joined the Teutonic Order
1 CONT     * Albert, Bishop of Halberstadt (died 1358)
1 CONT     * Henry, Bishop of Hildesheim (died 1362)
1 CONT     * Otto (died 1344)
1 CONT     * Magnus (died 1369)
1 CONT     * Ernest (died 1367)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Allgemeine Deutsche Biographie, vol. 1, p. 261-263
1 CONT
1 CONT     * At the House of Welf site
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Albert the Tall Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, Prince of Wolfenbüttel
1 CONT 1279–1286 Succeeded by
1 CONT William I
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT none Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, Prince of Göttingen
1 CONT 1286–1318 Succeeded by
1 CONT Otto the Mild
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT William I Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, Prince of Wolfenbüttel
1 CONT 1292–1318 Succeeded by
1 CONT Otto the Mild
0 @N6000000002188018318@ NOTE Magnus the Pious, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Magnus (died 1369), called the Pious (Latin Pius), was duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Albert the Fat, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Magnus was still a minor when his father died in 1318; he and his brother Ernest were put under the guardianship of their elder brother Otto, who continued as sole ruler even 
1 CONC after his brothers came of age. After marrying Sophie, a niece of Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor, Magnus was appointed margrave of Landsberg and count palatine of Saxony by the Emperor in 1333. Magnus took residence at Sangerhausen.
1 CONC  When Otto died in 1344, Magnus and Ernest jointly took over government of the state; but already on 17 April 1345, they agreed to divide the territory. Magnus received the Principality of Wolfenbüttel.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1346, a border war between Wolfenbüttel and the Archbishop of Magdeburg broke out. In exchange for help in this conflict, Magnus sold the Margraviate of Landsberg to Frederick II, Margrave of Meißen. But the Archbishop conquere
1 CONC d Schöningen in 1347, and Magnus had to cede Hötensleben and some other possessions to the Archbishop. Financially ruined by the war, Magnus could not stop the cities in the state from acquiring more and more rights; especially th
1 CONC e City of Brunswick was becoming more powerful.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1348, the Emperor gave Landsberg and the Palatinate of Saxony to Bernard, Prince of Anhalt. The ensuing conflict over these territories between Magnus and Bernard ended amicably with arriage between Magnus' son Magnus and Cathe
1 CONC rine, daughter of Valdemar, Prince of Anhalt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Magnus attempted to secure the Principality of Lüneburg for his son Louis, so that it could be reunited with Wolfenbüttel. The prince of Lüneburg, William II, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, a member of the same house to which Magnus 
1 CONC belonged, the House of Welf, did not have sons; however, he had already promised the principality to a son of his daughter, a relative of the Duke of Saxony, before he agreed to Magnus' plan. Louis then married William's daughter 
1 CONC Matilda. A lengthy conflict broke out that culminated in the Lüneburg Succession War, which was resolved only in 1388.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1367, Magnus joined Dietrich, Archbishop of Magdeburg, Albert, Bishop of Halberstadt, Valdemar, Prince of Anhalt, and others in a campaign against Gerhard, Bishop of Hildesheim; they were defeated by Hildesheim in a battle near
1 CONC  Farmsen and Dinklar on 3 September. Magnus was taken prisoner, and had to buy his freedom. He died in Summer of 1369.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 1327, Magnus married Sophie (died 1356), daughter of Henry I, Margrave of Brandenburg. They had the following children that reached adulthood:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Magnus (died 1373)
1 CONT     * Louis (died 1367)
1 CONT     * Albert, Archbishop of Bremen (died 1395)
1 CONT     * Henry, Provost of Halberstadt Cathedral
1 CONT     * Ernest
1 CONT     * Matilda, married Bernard III, Prince of Anhalt
1 CONT     * Helen, married Otto, Count of Hoya
1 CONT     * Agnes, married Eric, Count of Hoya
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Allgemeine Deutsche Biographie, vol. 20, p. 62-64
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Zedlers Universal-Lexicon, vol. 19, p. 240-241
1 CONT
1 CONT     * at the House of Welf site
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Otto the Mild Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, Prince of Wolfenbüttel
1 CONT 1344–1369 Succeeded by
1 CONT Magnus with the Necklace
